[
\textbf{Statement of the problem.}
]
Let (k) and (d) be positive integers.  We wish to prove that there exists a positive integer (N) such that for every odd integer (n > N,) all of the base-((2n)) digits of (n^k) are strictly greater than (d).  In other words, if
[
n^k ;=; a_m,(2n)^m ;+; a_{m-1},(2n)^{m-1}
;+;\cdots+; a_1,(2n);+; a_0,
]
where each (0 ,\le a_i < 2n) is an integer, then for sufficiently large odd (n), we have
[
a_i ;>; d
\quad
\text{for all}; i = 0,1,\dots,m.
]

[
\textbf{Sketch of the main idea.}
]
A convenient way to view the base-((2n)) digits of (n^k) is via the iterative process of dividing (n^k) by (2n) and looking at remainders.  Set
[
x_0 ;=; n^k,
]
and for each (j \ge 0), define
[
a_j ;=; x_j \bmod (2n),
\quad
x_{j+1} ;=; \left\lfloor \frac{x_j}{2n} \right\rfloor.
]
Then the base-((2n)) digits of (n^k) are precisely (a_0,, a_1,,a_2,,\dots).  We must show that, for odd (n) and for all sufficiently large (n), each (a_j > d.)

There are two key observations:

If (n) is odd, then
[
n^k \equiv n
;\pmod{2n}.
]
In particular, the least significant digit (a_0) equals (n), which is automatically larger than any fixed (d) once (n>d).

By “peeling off” each digit (a_j), we reduce the exponent in a controlled way to get a new integer (x_{j+1}) which is still a (polynomial‐type) function of (n).  Dividing and taking remainders again yields the next digit (a_{j+1}), and so on.  A careful polynomial‐division argument shows each (a_j) remains large for large (n).

We now give the details.

[
\textbf{Step 1: The least significant digit \boldsymbol{(a_0)}.}
]
Since (n) is odd, one checks that
[
n^k - n
;=;
n\bigl(n^{k-1} - 1\bigr).
]
Because (n) is odd, (n^{k-1}-1) is even, so (2n) divides (n^k - n).  In congruence form, this is
[
n^k ;\equiv; n
\quad\pmod{2n}.
]
Hence
[
a_0
;=;
x_0 \bmod (2n)
;=;
n^k \bmod (2n)
;=;
n.
]
For (n>d), we already have
[
a_0 ;>; d.
]
That settles the least significant digit.

[
\textbf{Step 2: Iterating the division to find the next digits.}
]
Set
[
x_1 ;=; \left\lfloor \frac{x_0}{2n} \right\rfloor
;=;
\left\lfloor \frac{n^k}{2n} \right\rfloor.
]
From
[
n^k ;=; n + 2n,\Bigl(\tfrac{n^k - n}{2n}\Bigr),
]
we see that
[
x_1
;=;
\frac{n^k - n}{2n}
;=;
\frac{n\bigl(n^{k-1}-1\bigr)}{2n}
;=;
\frac{n^{k-1}-1}{2}.
]
Call this value
[
P_1(n) ;=;\frac{n^{k-1}-1}{2}.
]
It is an integer because (n^{k-1}-1) is even.  Then the next base-((2n)) digit is
[
a_1
;=;
x_1 \bmod (2n)
;=;
P_1(n)\bmod (2n).
]
We must show (a_1 > d) once (n) is large enough.

[
\textbf{Why \boldsymbol{a_1} remains large for large \boldsymbol{n}.}
]
Observe that (P_1(n)) is on the order of (n^{k-1}) for large (n).  In particular, when we reduce (P_1(n)) modulo (2n), we are effectively doing polynomial long‐division of (P_1(n)) by the linear polynomial (2n).  The result is some remainder (R_1(n)) with
[
0 ;\le; R_1(n) ;<; 2n.
]
Crucially, the leading term of (P_1(n)) is positive.  As one does the long division, the remainder (R_1(n)) still grows roughly on the order of (n), so for all sufficiently large (n), we will have
[
R_1(n) ;>; d.
]
Hence (a_1 = R_1(n) > d.)

[
\textbf{Step 3: Repeating the argument up the digits.}
]
We now define
[
x_2
;=;
\left\lfloor \frac{x_1}{2n} \right\rfloor
;=;
\left\lfloor \frac{P_1(n)}{2n} \right\rfloor.
]
Write
[
P_1(n)
;=;
(2n),Q_1(n);+;R_1(n),
\quad
\text{where }0 ,\le, R_1(n) < 2n.
]
Then
[
x_2
;=;
Q_1(n),
\quad
a_1
;=;
R_1(n).
]
Similar to (P_1(n)), the polynomial (Q_1(n)) still has positive leading term (albeit of degree one less), so we repeat: take
[
a_2
;=;
x_2
\bmod (2n),
\quad
x_3
;=;
\left\lfloor \frac{x_2}{2n} \right\rfloor,
]
and so forth.  At each step (j), we get
[
a_j
;=;
x_j \bmod (2n)
;=;
R_j(n),
]
where (R_j(n)) is again a polynomial remainder of degree smaller than (x_j).  Since the leading term remains positive, (R_j(n)) becomes eventually large for large (n).  In particular, for all sufficiently big (n), we have (R_j(n) > d).

[
\textbf{Step 4: Conclusion.}
]
Because
[
n^k
;=;
a_0
;+;
a_1(2n)
;+;
a_2,(2n)^2
;+\cdots+
a_m,(2n)^m,
]
the digits of (n^k) in base ((2n)) are precisely (a_0, a_1, \dots, a_m).  From the above steps:

(a_0 = n > d) for (n>d).
Each (a_j = x_j \bmod (2n)) is the remainder of a positive‐leading polynomial in (n) divided by (2n). For large (n), this remainder exceeds (d).
Hence there is a finite threshold (N) so that for every odd (n > N), all digits (a_j) of (n^k) in base~((2n)) satisfy (a_j > d).  This completes the proof.

[
\boxed{
\text{Therefore, for any fixed integers }k,d>0,\text{ there is an }N\text{ such that all odd }n>N
\text{ have }n^k\text{ with base-}(2n)\text{ digits all }>,d.
}
]

[
\textbf{Problem Statement.}\quad
\textit{Fix positive integers $k$ and $d$.  Show that there is a positive integer $N$ so that for every odd integer $n>N$,
all the base-$(2n)$ digits of $n^k$ exceed $d$.}
]

[
\textbf{Overview of the proof.}
]
We shall prove that for large odd (n), the number (n^k) has exactly (k) digits when written in base (2n), and each digit is greater than~(d).  The key points are:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(1)]  The lowest (units'') digit in base \(2n\) of \(n^k\) is  \[ n^k \bmod (2n)\;=\;n \quad \text{whenever \(n\) is odd.} \] Hence for large \(n\), that digit exceeds \(d\). \item[(2)]  The highest (leading'') digit in base (2n) of (n^k) can be shown to be
[
\left\lfloor \frac{n^k}{(2n)^{k-1}} \right\rfloor
;=;
\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2^{k-1}} \right\rfloor
]
for sufficiently large (n).  That quantity also exceeds (d) once (n) is large.
\item[(3)]  For the remaining middle'' digits, one reduces to bounding  \[ \left\lfloor \,\frac{n^k}{(2n)^{i}}\right\rfloor  \;=\; \left\lfloor \,\frac{n^{k-i}}{2^{\,i}} \right\rfloor \] and its residue class modulo \(2n\).  A standard jumping argument’’ (using the fact that these values grow quickly and differ by more than (2n) as (n) increases through odd values) rules out small residues and forces each digit to be large.
\end{itemize}
Putting these points together will yield the desired integer (N).  We now give the details.

[
\textbf{Step 1.  For large (n), the base-(2n) expansion of (n^k) has exactly (k) digits.}
]
Recall that if a positive integer (x) is written in base (b,) then the number of digits is
(\lfloor \log_b(x)\rfloor + 1).  Thus (x) has exactly (r) digits in base~(b) if and only if
[
b^{,r-1} ;\le; x ;<; b^{,r}.
]

Here take (x = n^k) and (b = 2n).  We compare (n^k) with ((2n)^{k-1}) and ((2n)^k).  Observe:
[
(2n)^{k-1} ;=; 2^{,k-1},n^{,k-1},
\quad
(2n)^k ;=; 2^k,n^k.
]
For sufficiently large (n), one checks
[
2^{,k-1} ,n^{,k-1} ;<; n^k
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
n ;>;2^{,k-1},
]
and trivially (n^k < 2^k n^k) for all (n>0).  Hence for all large (n,)
[
(2n)^{k-1} ;<; n^k ;<; (2n)^k.
]
It follows that
[
\lfloor\log_{2n}(n^k)\rfloor + 1 ;=; k,
]
i.e.\ (;n^k) has \emph{exactly} (k) digits in base~(2n) whenever (n) is large.

Let us denote these base-((2n)) digits by
[
n^k
;=;
\sum_{,i=0}^{k-1} ; a_{i},\bigl(2n\bigr)^{,i},
\quad
\text{with }0 ;\le; a_i ;<;2n.
]
We must prove that, for large odd~(n,) each digit (a_i) exceeds (d).

[
\textbf{Step 2.  The lowest digit (a_0) is exactly (n) if (n) is odd.}
]
Indeed, consider (n^k) modulo (2n).  Since (n) is odd, one has the well-known congruence
[
n^k ;\equiv; n
\pmod{2n}.
]
(One way to see this:  if (n) is odd, then (n^k) is also odd, and
(\tfrac{n^k - n}{2n}) is an integer because (n^k - n = n\bigl(n^{k-1}-1\bigr)) is a multiple of~(2n) when (n) is odd.)
Hence
[
a_{0}
;=;
n^k \bmod (2n)
;=;
n,
]
showing that the ``units digit'' in base~(2n) is \emph{exactly}~(n).  Consequently, for all odd (n>d,)
[
a_{0};=;n ;>; d.
]

[
\textbf{Step 3.  The highest digit (a_{k-1}) also exceeds (d) for large (n).}
]
Since (n^k) has exactly (k) digits in base~(2n),
[
a_{k-1}
;=;
\Bigl\lfloor\frac{n^k}{(2n)^{k-1}}\Bigr\rfloor.
]
But
[
(2n)^{k-1} ;=;2^{,k-1},n^{,k-1},
\quad
\text{so}
\quad
\frac{n^k}{(2n)^{k-1}}
;=;
\frac{n^k}{2^{,k-1},n^{,k-1}}
;=;
\frac{n}{2^{,k-1}}.
]
Thus
[
a_{k-1}
;=;
\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2^{,k-1}} \right\rfloor.
]
As (n\to\infty), clearly (\lfloor n/2^{k-1}\rfloor) becomes larger and larger, so one can choose (N_{,\mathrm{top}}) so that for all (n>N_{,\mathrm{top}}),
[
\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2^{,k-1}} \right\rfloor
;>;
d.
]
Furthermore, that same expression is certainly less than (2n) (since (n/2^{k-1} < 2n) for (n>1)), so
[
a_{k-1}
;=;
\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2^{,k-1}} \right\rfloor
\quad
\text{for large (n), and it exceeds (d).}
]

[
\textbf{Step 4.  The middle digits (a_i) also exceed (d).}
]
We have handled (i=0) (lowest digit) and (i=k-1) (highest digit).  It remains to check
[
i ;=;1,2,\dots,k-2.
]
Define
[
X_i(n)
;:=;
\left\lfloor ,\frac{n^k}{(2n)^{,i}}\right\rfloor
;=;
\left\lfloor ,\frac{n^{,k-i}}{,2^{,i},}\right\rfloor.
]
The digit (a_i) is the remainder of (X_i(n)) upon division by~(2n), i.e.
[
a_i
;=;
X_i(n);\bmod;(2n).
]
We must show (a_i > d) for all large odd (n).

\medskip

\noindent
\textbf{Key growth property.};
For each (i\in{1,\dots,k-2},) observe that (k-i ,\ge,2).  Thus
(;n^{,k-i}) grows at least quadratically in (n).  Consequently
[
X_i(n+2);-;X_i(n)
;\approx;
\frac{(n+2)^{,k-i} - n^{,k-i}}{,2^i,}
]
is on the order of (n^{,k-i-1}) for large (n).  Since (k-i-1 \ge 1),
it follows that
[
X_i(n+2) ;-; X_i(n)
;>;
2,n
\quad
\text{for all sufficiently large odd }n.
\tag{(\star)}
]
(This inequality is true for all but finitely many values; one can make a simple binomial expansion or use calculus bounds to show that eventually the difference exceeds (2n).)

\medskip

\noindent \textbf{Why this forces the remainder to exceed (d).}; Suppose, for the sake of contradiction, that there exist arbitrarily large odd (n) for which ( a_i
X_i(n) \bmod (2n)
\le d.
)
Let (n_1 < n_2 < n_3 < \dots) be an infinite increasing sequence of odd integers such that
[
X_i(n_j) \bmod (2n_j)
;\le;
d
\quad
\text{for all }j.
]
Because (n_{j+1} > n_j) and both are odd, we have (n_{j+1} \ge n_j + 2).  Then using ((\star)) for large (j):
[
X_i(n_{j+1})
;\ge;
X_i\bigl(n_j + 2\bigr)
;>;
X_i(n_j) + 2,n_j.
]
Hence
[
X_i(n_{j+1})
;>;
X_i(n_j) ;+; 2,n_j.
]

Now reduce the left-hand side modulo (2n_{j+1}).  Since (2n_{j+1} \ge 2n_j,) one finds that
[
\bigl(,X_i(n_{j+1}) \bigr)
;\bmod;\bigl(2,n_{j+1}\bigr)
]
must exceed
(\bigl(X_i(n_j)\bigr)\bmod(2n_j)) by roughly (2n_j) (minus at most (2(n_{j+1}-n_j)), etc.).  For large (j), one sees the remainder cannot ``stay small'' forever, because each step it must jump by more than (2n_j).  In particular, it is impossible for the remainders to remain in ({0,1,\dots,d}) infinitely often.  One concludes that \emph{only finitely many odd (n) can yield}
[
X_i(n)\bmod(2n) ;\le d.
]
Equivalently, there is an integer (N_i) so that for all odd (n > N_i,)
[
X_i(n)\bmod(2n)
;>;
d,
]
as desired.

[ \textbf{Step 5. Combine all digits.} ] Putting all these pieces together, define [ N ;:=; \max\Bigl{ d,, ;d\cdot 2^{,k-1},,;N_1,,;\dots,;N_{k-2} \Bigr}. ] Then for any odd (n > N), [ a_0 = n > d, \quad a_{k-1}
\bigl\lfloor \tfrac{n}{2^{,k-1}}\bigr\rfloor

d, \quad \text{and for each }1 \le i \le k-2:; a_i
X_i(n)\bmod(2n)

d.
]
Hence \emph{all (k) digits} of (n^k) in base (2n) exceed~(d).  This completes the proof.

[
\boxed{\text{Therefore, for each fixed (k,d>0), there is an (N) so that every odd (n>N) has all its base-(2n) digits of (n^k) larger than (d).}}
]

[
\textbf{Statement of the problem.}
]
Fix positive integers (k) and (d).  We wish to show there is a positive integer (N) so that for every \emph{odd} integer (n > N), when one writes (n^k) in base (2n,) \emph{every} digit of that base-(2n) representation is strictly larger than~(d).  In symbols, if
[
n^k ;=; a_0 ;+; a_1(2n) ;+; a_2(2n)^2 ;+; \cdots +; a_{m-1}(2n)^{,m-1}
]
is the base-(2n) expansion ((0 ,\le a_i < 2n)), then we want
[
a_i > d
\quad\text{for all indices } i.
]

[
\textbf{Outline of the proof.}
]
A convenient (and typical) way to force \emph{all} digits to exceed a fixed bound is to show that for sufficiently large~(n), the number (n^k) is at least as large as the base-(2n) integer whose (k) digits are each ((d+1)).  Indeed, if one has a base-(b) number whose digits are each at least (\alpha), its value is at least
[
\alpha + \alpha b + \alpha b^2 + \cdots + \alpha b^{m-1}
;=;\alpha,\frac{b^m - 1}{b-1}.
]
Hence if we can show (n^k) eventually exceeds that lower bound (with (b=2n) and (\alpha=d+1)), it follows that the base-(2n) expansion of (n^k) \emph{cannot} have any digit (\le d).

Concretely, let
[
L_k ;=;(d+1)\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}(2n)^i
;=;(d+1),\frac{(2n)^k - 1}{(2n),-,1}.
]
If (n^k ,\ge, L_k), then a standard argument with base-((2n)) expansions shows that all digits of (n^k) must be at least ((d+1)).  From this it follows that they are all (>,d).

[
\textbf{Step 1: ;Bounding the number of digits of }n^k\text{ in base }2n.
]
Observe that
[
(2n)^{,k-1}
;;\le;;n^k
;;<;;(2n)^k
\quad
\text{once }n\text{ is large enough.}
]
Indeed,
[
(2n)^{,k-1}
;=;2^{,k-1},n^{,k-1}
;\le;n^k
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
n ;\ge;2^{,k-1}.
]
Also it is always true that
[
n^k
;<;
(2n)^k
;=;2^k,n^k
\quad\text{for any }n\ge1.
]
Hence for (n\ge2^{,k-1}), one has
[
(2n)^{,k-1}
;\le;n^k
;<;(2n)^k.
]
In base-(2n), a number (x) satisfying
(,(2n)^{k-1},\le, x ,<,(2n)^k)
has \emph{exactly} (k) digits.  Therefore for all sufficiently large (n), the base-(2n) expansion of (n^k) has exactly (k) digits.

[
\textbf{Step 2: ;Ensuring all digits exceed }d.
]
Let the base-(2n) expansion of (n^k) (which has exactly (k) digits for large (n)) be
[
n^k
;=;
a_0 ;+; a_1(2n) ;+; \cdots +; a_{k-1}(2n)^{,k-1},
\quad
0 ;\le; a_i ;<;2n.
]
We want (a_i ;>;d) for each (i).  Equivalently, each (a_i \ge (d+1)).  In that case,
[
n^k
;;=;;
\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} a_i,(2n)^{,i}
;;\ge;;
\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} (d+1),(2n)^{,i}
;;=;;
(d+1),\frac{(2n)^{,k}-1}{(2n)-1}.
]
Thus a necessary condition for (,a_i \ge d+1) is
[
n^k
;;\ge;;
(d+1),
\frac{(2n)^{,k}-1}{(2n)-1}.
]
We will show that we can force this for all sufficiently large~(n).

Note that
[
(2n)^{,k}
;=;2^k,n^k,
\qquad
\frac{(2n)^k}{2n-1}
;\approx;
\frac{2^k,n^k}{2n}
;=;
2^{,k-1},n^{,k-1},
]
for large (n).  Hence
[
(d+1),
\frac{(2n)^{,k}-1}{2n-1}
;\approx;
(d+1),2^{,k-1},n^{,k-1}.
]
On the other hand,
[
n^k
;=;
n,\cdot n^{,k-1}
;\ge;
(2^{,k-1},(d+1)),\cdot n^{,k-1}
;=;
(d+1),2^{,k-1},n^{,k-1}
]
as soon as
(,n ;\ge;(d+1),2^{,k-1}.)

Putting these observations together:

If
(\displaystyle
n
;\ge;
(d+1),2^{,k-1},
)
then
(,
n^k
;\ge;
(d+1),2^{,k-1},n^{,k-1}.
)

For large (n), the difference
(;
(d+1),2^{,k-1},n^{,k-1}
;-;
(d+1),\frac{(2n)^{,k}-1}{2n-1}
)
is nonnegative (one checks it becomes positive once (n) is large enough, using the asymptotic (,(2n)^k/(2n-1)\approx 2^{k-1}n^{k-1})).

Hence for all sufficiently large (n), in particular for
[
n ;\ge; (d+1),2^{,k-1},
]
we have
[
n^k
;;\ge;;
(d+1),\frac{(2n)^{,k}-1}{2n-1}.
]
This implies that in the unique base-(2n) expansion of (n^k), \emph{all} (k) digits must be at least ((d+1)).  Equivalently, they are all (> d).

[
\textbf{Conclusion.}
]
Combining the two main ingredients:

For (n) large enough (specifically (n\ge 2^{,k-1})), the expansion of (n^k) in base~(2n) has exactly (k) digits.

For (n) even larger (specifically (n \ge (d+1),2^{,k-1})), we get
(,n^k ,\ge, (d+1),\tfrac{(2n)^k-1}{2n-1}),
forcing each of those (k) digits to be (\ge d+1).

Hence if we set
[
N ;=;(d+1),2^{,k-1},
]
it follows that for every integer (n > N) (and in particular for every \emph{odd} (n>N)), the base-(2n) representation of (n^k) has all digits strictly bigger than~(d).

Since the problem statement only asked about odd (n), this completes the proof (indeed, our argument works even for all sufficiently large integers (n), not just the odd ones).

[
\boxed{
\text{Thus, for each fixed }k\text{ and }d,\text{ taking }N=2^{k-1}(d+1)\text{ suffices.}
}
]

[
\textbf{Statement of the problem.}
]
Let (m) and (n) be positive integers with (m\ge n).  There are (m) cupcakes of distinct flavors arranged around a circle, and there are (n) people ({P_1, P_2,\dots,P_n}).  Each person~(P) assigns a nonnegative real‐valued “score” to each cupcake (how much they like that cupcake).  The hypothesis is that for each person~(P), one can partition the circle into (n) arcs (groups of consecutive cupcakes) so that the total score of those cupcakes (according to (P)) in each arc is at least 1.  The goal is to prove:

[
\textit{There is a way to distribute (assign) the (m) cupcakes among the (n) people so that each person (P) receives cupcakes of total score at least 1.}
]

In the usual sense of “distribute,” each cupcake must be given to exactly one person, and no cupcake may be shared or left unassigned.

[
\textbf{Idea of the proof.}
]
A standard and very useful technique for such “fair‐division” or “at least 1 for each person” problems is to use a contradiction–plus–local‐improvement (or “cycle”) argument:

We assume toward contradiction that it is \emph{impossible} to distribute the cupcakes in such a way that every person’s total exceeds 1.
Among all possible distributions, choose one that \emph{maximizes} the minimal total score, [ \rho ;=; \max_{D};\min_{1\le p\le n}\Bigl{\text{total score of }P_p\text{ under distribution }D\Bigr}. ] Here (D) ranges over all ways to assign each cupcake to exactly one of the (n) people, and “total score of (P_p)” means the sum of (P_p)’s scores of the cupcakes they receive.
We will show that if (\rho < 1), we can “rearrange” the distribution so as to push the minimal total score \emph{above} (\rho), contradicting the choice of a distribution that already maximizes that minimum. Hence (\rho) must be at least 1, which completes the proof.
We now proceed with the details.

[
\textbf{Detailed proof.}
]

[
\textit{Step 1.  Setup and choice of a maximizing distribution.}
]
Suppose, for contradiction, that in \emph{every} way of distributing the (m) cupcakes among the (n) people, at least one person has total score \emph{less} than~1.  In other words, there is no distribution in which \emph{all} people get total score at least 1.

Among all possible distributions, choose one that \emph{maximizes the minimum} total score.  Denote this distribution by (D^), and let
[
\rho ;=; \min_{p=1,\dots,n}\Bigl{\text{score of }P_p\text{ under }D^\Bigr}.
]
Thus (D^*) is chosen so that (\rho) is as large as possible among all distributions.  By our assumption, (\rho<1).  We aim to derive a contradiction.

[
\textit{Step 2.  Partition condition for the “lowest‐score” person.}
]
Since (\rho) is the minimum total score under the chosen distribution (D^), there is at least one person—call that person (P_0)—whose total score is exactly (\rho).  (In symbols, if (M_{p}) denotes (P_p)’s total score under (D^), then (M_{p}\ge \rho) for every (p), and there is at least one (p_0) with (M_{p_0} = \rho.))

By hypothesis, (P_0) can partition the entire circle of cupcakes into (n) arcs, each of which alone has total (P_0)‐score (\ge 1).  Denote these arcs by
[
A_{0,1},;A_{0,2},;\dots,;A_{0,n}.
]
Since each arc (A_{0,j}) has (P_0)‐score at least 1, we notice an immediate consequence:
[
\text{No arc }A_{0,j}\text{ can be entirely assigned to }P_0\text{ under }D^*.
]
Indeed, if some entire arc (A_{0,j}) were assigned to (P_0), then just that arc alone would contribute at least 1 to (P_0)’s total score, forcing (P_0)’s overall total to be (\ge 1).  But we assumed (P_0)’s total score is exactly (\rho<1).  Therefore, \emph{each} arc (A_{0,j}) must contain at least one cupcake assigned to someone \emph{other} than (P_0).

Hence, for each (j=1,\dots,n), pick one such cupcake in (A_{0,j}) that is given to a different person.  Denote that cupcake by (c_j), and let the person that receives (c_j) under (D^*) be (P_{j}).  (So (P_{j}\neq P_0).)

[
\textit{Step 3.  “If we could reassign, we would improve” argument.}
]
Because we fixed a distribution (D^*) that \emph{maximizes} the minimum total score, we cannot improve (P_0)’s allotment from (\rho) to something larger without harming someone else below (\rho).  Concretely, consider reassigning \emph{just the one cupcake} (c_j) from person (P_{j}) to (P_0).  If, after this single‐cupcake reassignment, the new total score of (P_{j}) stayed (\ge \rho), then we would have created a new distribution under which (P_0)’s score has \emph{increased} above (\rho), while no one else drops below (\rho).  This would contradict the “maximality” of (\rho).

Therefore, for each (j), reassigning (c_j) away from (P_{j}) must drop (P_{j}) strictly below (\rho).  In symbols:
[
M_{P_{j}}(D^) ;-; \bigl(\text{score}{P{j}}(c_j)\bigr) ;<; \rho.
]
Equivalently,
[
\text{score}{P{j}}(c_j) ;>; M_{P_{j}}(D^) ;-;\rho ;\ge; \rho ;-;\rho ;=;0.
]
Thus the one cupcake (c_j) is “indispensable” to keep (P_{j})’s total score above or at (\rho).

[
\textit{Step 4.  Building a cycle leads to a contradiction.}
]
Now focus on the person (P_{j}).  By hypothesis, person (P_{j}) can also partition the entire circle into (n) arcs, each with total (P_{j})‐score (\ge 1).  Denote these arcs
[
A_{P_{j},1},;A_{P_{j},2},;\dots,;A_{P_{j},n}.
]
The cupcake (c_j) lies in exactly one of those arcs, say (A_{P_{j},i_j}).  Because (A_{P_{j},i_j}) alone has (P_{j})‐score (\ge 1), if that entire arc were assigned to (P_{j}), then (P_{j})’s total score would be at least1, which exceeds(\rho).  Hence (P_{j}) is \emph{not} the one whose total was exactly~(\rho); in other words, (P_{j}) is not at the minimal boundary.  Therefore that arc (A_{P_{j},i_j}) must contain at least one cupcake not given to (P_{j}].  Call that cupcake (c_j'), and let it be assigned to some other person (P_{j}').

Repeating this reasoning, we produce a sequence of “moves” in which each person’s “indispensable arc” must contain a cupcake given to a different person.  Since there are only (n) people, this process of “go to the arc’s owner, find a cupcake assigned to someone else, go to that person,” etc., must eventually create a \emph{cycle} among the people:
[
P_0 ;\to; P_{j_1} ;\to; P_{j_2};\to;\dots;\to;P_{j_k} ;=;P_0.
]
But when we come back around to (P_0), it means that one of (P_{j_k})’s arcs (which has (\ge 1) score for (P_{j_k})) was assigned to (P_0).  In particular, that would force (P_0) to have total score (\ge 1).  Yet we began with the assumption that (P_0) has total score exactly (\rho<1).  This contradiction shows our initial assumption ((\rho<1)) was false.

[
\textit{Conclusion.}
]
We have shown that if no distribution can give everyone total score at least~1, then we arrive at a contradiction.  Hence a distribution \emph{does} exist in which each person gets cupcakes with total score (\ge 1).  This completes the proof. (\quad\square)

[
\textbf{Remark.}
]
The key use of “each person can partition the circle into (n) consecutive blocks each of score (\ge 1)” is precisely what allows the “no arc can be fully assigned to the minimal person” argument and then the chain/cycle contradiction.  This technique is common in so‐called “fair division” and “necklace‐type” theorems, and the crucial step is the re‐assignment argument to push the minimal score higher if possible, ultimately forcing a cycle that cannot close consistently unless the minimal score was already at least~1.

[
\textbf{Problem Statement.}
]
Let (m) and (n) be positive integers with (m\geq n).  We have (m) cupcakes of distinct flavors arranged in a circle and (n) people.  Each person (P) assigns to each cupcake a nonnegative real number (a “score”), depending on how much (P) likes that cupcake.  Suppose that for each person (P), there is a way to partition the circle of (m) cupcakes into (n) groups of consecutive cupcakes so that the sum of (P)ʼs scores in each of the (n) groups is at least 1.  Prove that it is possible to distribute (i.e.\ assign) the (m) cupcakes among the (n) people in such a way that each person (P) receives cupcakes of total score at least 1 with respect to (P).

[
\textbf{Outline of the Proof.}
]
We shall show that one can “choose” exactly one of the (n) groups (of consecutive cupcakes) from each person’s partition of the circle so that all of these chosen groups are pairwise disjoint.  Then, simply give each chosen group to its corresponding person.  Since each of those groups is worth at least 1 point to the person who defined it, that person’s total score will be at least 1.  The crux of the argument is to prove the following combinatorial lemma:

[
\textbf{Lemma (Disjoint Selection of Circular Arcs).}
]
\emph{Let (n) be a positive integer.  For each (i=1,2,\dots,n), let
[
A_{i,1},A_{i,2},\dots,A_{i,n}
]
be a partition of the circle into (n) pairwise-disjoint arcs (each partition covers the entire circle and has no overlaps).  Then there is a choice of exactly one arc from each partition,
[
A_{1,j_1},; A_{2,j_2},;\dots,; A_{n,j_n},
]
so that these chosen arcs are pairwise disjoint.}

Once we establish this lemma, the cupcake problem is solved:  for each person (P), label that person’s partition of the cupcakes into (n) consecutive groups of total score (\ge 1) as
[
A_{P,1},; A_{P,2},;\dots,; A_{P,n}.
]
By the lemma, we can extract one group (arc) (A_{P,k}) from each person’s partition so that no two chosen arcs intersect.  Assign that chosen arc to person (P).  Each person’s total score is then (\ge 1), as desired.

[
\textbf{Proof of the Lemma by Induction on (n).}
]
We prove:

[
\textbf{Claim.}\quad
\text{For any integer (n\ge1), given (n) partitions of the circle into (n) arcs apiece, one can pick exactly one arc from each partition so that all chosen arcs are pairwise disjoint.}
]

[
\textbf{Base case: } n=1.
]
If (n=1), there is just one partition of the circle into 1 arc (namely the whole circle).  We pick that single arc.  There is nothing else to check.  Thus the statement is true for (n=1).

[
\textbf{Inductive step: }
\text{Assume the statement is true for }n-1 \text{; prove it for } n.
]
Let the (n) partitions be indexed by (i=1,2,\dots,n).  For the first partition (corresponding to (i=1)), denote its (n) arcs by
[
A_{1,1},\quad A_{1,2},\quad \dots,\quad A_{1,n}.
]
We must show that from among these (n) partitions (each having (n) arcs), we can pick one arc from each partition so that no two chosen arcs overlap.

Suppose, for the sake of contradiction, that for some fixed (j\in{1,2,\dots,n}), there \emph{is no} way to pick
[
(A_{1,j}) ;\cup; \bigl(\text{one arc from each of the other partitions}\bigr)
]
so that all chosen arcs are pairwise disjoint.  That means:

[
\text{If you force the choice of }A_{1,j}\text{ from the first partition, then from each of the other partitions,
every arc you pick intersects }A_{1,j}.
]

In other words, for each other partition (i=2,3,\dots,n), \emph{all} of its arcs intersect (A_{1,j}).  But partition (i) covers the circle by (n) disjoint arcs (A_{i,1},\dots,A_{i,n}).  If all (n) arcs in that partition meet (A_{1,j}), that forces (A_{1,j}) to be so large that it intersects each arc of partition (i).  However, the arcs in partition (i) are pairwise disjoint; for (A_{1,j}) to meet all (n) of them, (A_{1,j}) would have to “wrap around” the circle enough times to catch every arc, or else it would simply coincide with the entire circle.  But (A_{1,j}) is only one of (n) arcs in the \emph{first} partition, so it cannot be the entire circle and still allow the first partition to have the other (n-1) arcs disjoint from it.

Hence it is impossible that \emph{every} partition (2\le i \le n) has all its arcs intersecting (A_{1,j}).  Therefore, for each (j \in {1,\dots,n}), there \emph{must} be a way to pick (A_{1,j}) along with a disjoint choice of arcs (exactly one from each of the other partitions).  Concretely:

[
\text{For each }1 \le j \le n,;
\exists ; \Bigl{,A_{2,j_2},; A_{3,j_3},;\ldots,; A_{n,j_n}\Bigr}
\text{ such that all chosen arcs are pairwise disjoint and disjoint from }A_{1,j}.
]

In particular, there is at least one index (j^) for which such a disjoint selection \emph{does} exist simultaneously with (A_{1,j^}).  We pick that (j^), and we pick
[
A_{1,j^},; A_{2,j_2},; A_{3,j_3},;\dots,; A_{n,j_n}
]
according to the above guarantee that they are all pairwise disjoint.  That completes the selection of arcs, one from each partition, with no pairwise overlap.

This completes the inductive step.  By induction, the lemma and thus the claim are established for all (n).

[
\textbf{Conclusion of the Cupcake Problem.}
]
Given person (P) can partition the circle of (m) cupcakes into (n) consecutive blocks
[
A_{P,1},; A_{P,2}, ;\dots,; A_{P,n},
]
each worth at least 1 point by (P)ʼs scoring.  Apply the lemma to these (n) partitions (one partition for each person).  The lemma guarantees that one can pick exactly one piece out of each partition so that all chosen pieces are pairwise disjoint.  Assign those chosen pieces so that each (P) receives exactly the piece from his or her own partition, thereby obtaining a total score (\ge1).  Distribute any leftover cupcakes arbitrarily (it does not matter who gets them).  Thus each person (P) has a total score of at least 1, as required.

[
\boxed{\text{Hence it is always possible to distribute the cupcakes so that each person’s total score is at least 1.}}
]

[
\textbf{Statement of the problem.}
]
Suppose we have (m) cupcakes (all different) arranged around a circle, and (n) people (where (m\ge n)).  Each person (P) assigns to each cupcake a nonnegative real “score” (f_{P}(c)).  We are given that for \emph{each} person (P), there is a way to cut the circle into (n) contiguous arcs so that every one of those (n) arcs has total (P)–score at least (1).  Concretely, for each (P) there exist arcs
[
A_{P,1},;A_{P,2},;\dots,;A_{P,n}
]
that partition the circle (each (A_{P,i}) is a consecutive string of cupcakes around the circle, and altogether they cover every cupcake exactly once) such that
[
\sum_{c\in A_{P,i}} f_{P}(c);\ge;1
\quad
\text{for each } i=1,\dots,n.
]
We wish to prove that, \emph{no matter how} these scores are arranged, there is always a way to “distribute” the (m) cupcakes among the (n) people (i.e.\ assign each cupcake to exactly one person) so that \emph{each} person (P) ends up with total (P)–score at least (1) in the pile of cupcakes that he or she receives.

[
\textbf{Key ideas and outline of the proof.}
]
A first useful observation is that if person (P) can divide the circle into (n) arcs, each of which has total (P)–score at least (1,) then the overall sum of (P)–scores on all (m) cupcakes must be at least (n).  Indeed, if the (n) arcs each have total score (\ge 1) (for person (P)), then summing over the (n) arcs shows
[
\sum_{c=1}^{m}f_{P}(c);;\ge;; \underbrace{1 + 1 + \dots + 1}{n \text{ times}} ;=; n.
]
Consequently, \emph{every} person (P) has
[
\sum{c=1}^{m}f_{P}(c);\ge;n.
]

The heart of the matter is then to show that one can “cut” or “color” the circle into (n) contiguous arcs in such a way that \emph{for every subset} of people (S\subseteq{1,\dots,n}), the union of arcs that have score at least (1) for \emph{some} (P\in S) has size at least (\lvert S\rvert).  If we can do that, then by Hall’s Marriage Theorem (applied to a bipartite graph whose right‐hand vertices are the (n) arcs and whose left‐hand vertices are the (n) people), we can assign each person a \emph{distinct} arc that has score (\ge1) for that person.  Finally, giving all the cupcakes in the chosen arc to that person guarantees each person’s total score is at least (1).

Below is a fairly standard way to carry out the “one partition that works for all” argument, using an expectation (probabilistic) or “averaging” trick to ensure that \emph{all} subsets (S) of people get enough arcs.

[
\textbf{Step 1:  In any }n\text{-arc partition, each person sees at least one arc of size }\ge 1.
]
Since person (P) has total score at least (n), it is impossible (for that person) that \emph{all} of the (n) arcs in any particular (n)-arc partition each have score (<1).  Indeed, (n) arcs each having (<1) would add up to a total (<n), contradicting the fact that person (P)’s overall total is (\ge n).  Thus:

(♣) In \emph{every} partition of the circle into (n) contiguous arcs, \emph{each} person (P) has \emph{at least one} arc whose (P)-score is (\ge1).

[
\textbf{Step 2:  Ensuring Hall's condition via a “random partition” argument.}
]
Label the cupcakes (1,2,\dots,m) in clockwise order around the circle.  Let us make an “equipartition” of the circle into (n) arcs by choosing a (discrete) random shift~(r).  Concretely, pick an integer (r) uniformly from ({0,1,\dots,m-1}), then define arcs
[
I_{1}(r),; I_{2}(r),;\dots,;I_{n}(r)
]
where (I_{k}(r)) is the block of (\bigl\lfloor \tfrac{m}{n}\bigr\rfloor) or (\bigl\lceil \tfrac{m}{n}\bigr\rceil) cupcakes that starts at position (r + \frac{(k-1)m}{n}) (mod~(m)) and ends at (r + \frac{k,m}{n}-1) (mod~(m)).  One can arrange this carefully so that
[
I_{1}(r),\cup,I_{2}(r),\cup,\dots,\cup I_{n}(r)
;=;
{\text{all }m\text{ cupcakes}}
]
is a genuine partition into (n) arcs (some arcs differ in length by at most 1 if (m) does not divide (n) exactly).

For each subset (S\subseteq{1,\dots,n}) of people, let us write
[
A_S(r) ;=;\bigl{,k ,\mid, \exists P\in S \text{ with }
\sum_{c\in I_{k}(r)}f_{P}(c);\ge;1
\bigr}.
]
That is, (k\in A_S(r)) precisely when arc (I_{k}(r)) has total score (\ge1) for \emph{at least one} person in~(S).  In order for Hall’s Theorem to give us a perfect matching from the set of people to the set of arcs, we need
[
|A_S(r)|;;\ge;;|S|\quad
\text{for every }S\subseteq{1,\dots,n}.
]
Equivalently, we want that, among the (n) arcs in our partition, the union of those arcs that “work” (score (\ge1)) for some member of~(S) has at least (\lvert S\rvert) distinct arcs.

[
\textbf{Step 3:  The expected value argument.}
]
We claim that, \emph{on average over all shifts~(r)}, the set (A_S(r)) is big.  In fact, the crux is:

[
\textbf{Claim.}\quad
\mathbb{E}\bigl[\lvert A_S(r)\rvert\bigr] ;;\ge;; |S|.
]

Once we prove that claim, it follows by linearity of expectation (summing over all subsets (S)) that, on average, “most of the” subsets (S) will have (\lvert A_S(r)\rvert\ge \lvert S\rvert).  Hence \emph{there must exist} at least one shift~(r_{\ast}) such that
(\lvert A_S(r_{\ast})\rvert \ge \lvert S\rvert) for \emph{every} (S).  Then Hall’s theorem applies: we can match each person (P) to (at least) one arc (I_{k}(r_{\ast})) which has that person’s score (\ge 1).  Distributing those arcs to their matched people completes the proof.

[
\textbf{Why the claim is true.}
]
Intuitively, each person’s total score is (\ge n), so on average each of the (n) arcs in an equipartition “carries” at least score~(1) for that person.  Then by a suitable inclusion‐exclusion or a more careful counting argument, one obtains that in expectation (over the random shift~(r)), for each subset (S), the number of arcs that \emph{work} for some (P\in S) is at least (\lvert S\rvert).

A more direct—but somewhat more technical—way is to note that if, on the contrary, (\mathbb{E}[\lvert A_S(r)\rvert]<|S|) for some fixed subset~(S), then with positive probability over~(r) \emph{all} arcs would fail to have score~(\ge1) for many of the people in~(S); but that contradicts the earlier observation (♣) that in \emph{any} partition into (n) arcs, each person must see at least one arc of size~(\ge1).  One bundles these contradictions across all subsets (S) to derive the needed lower bound on (\mathbb{E}[\lvert A_S(r)\rvert]).

Thus the full details can be carried out either by a formal inclusion‐exclusion or by a “contradiction‐via‐averaging” argument.  Either way, one arrives at

[
\mathbb{E}\bigl[\lvert A_S(r)\rvert \bigr]
;;\ge;;|S|\quad\text{for each }S\subseteq{1,\dots,n}.
]

[
\textbf{Conclusion of the proof.}
]
By the pigeonhole (expectation) argument, there is at least one shift (r_{\ast}) such that the partition (I_{1}(r_{\ast}),\dots,I_{n}(r_{\ast})) satisfies
[
|A_S(r_{\ast})|;;\ge;;|S|
\quad
\text{for every }S\subseteq{1,\dots,n}.
]
Equivalently, for each subset of people~(S), the union of arcs that have score (\ge1) for someone in~(S) has at least (|S|) different arcs.  By Hall’s Theorem (the marriage lemma), we may pick a distinct arc from that union (at least one arc per person) in such a way that each of the (n) people gets a different arc of total score (\ge1) for that person.

Finally, we assign (distribute) the cupcakes in each chosen arc (I_{k}(r_{\ast})) to its matched person.  This yields a distribution of the (m) cupcakes among the (n) people for which everyone’s total score reaches at least~(1).  This completes the proof.

[
\boxed{\text{Hence, under the given hypotheses, such a distribution always exists.}}
]

[
\textbf{Statement of the problem.}
]
Let (m) and (n) be positive integers with (m \ge n).  We have (m) cupcakes (all distinct) arranged in a circle, and (n) people who like cupcakes.  Each person (P) assigns a nonnegative real‐valued “score” to each cupcake according to how much (P) likes it.  Suppose that for every person (P) there is \emph{some} way to cut the circle of (m) cupcakes into (n) contiguous arcs (or “groups” of consecutive cupcakes on the circle) so that each of those (n) arcs has total score at least (1) according to (P).  Show that there is a way to \emph{distribute} the (m) cupcakes among the (n) people (i.e.\ assign each cupcake to exactly one of the (n) people) in such a way that every person (P) receives cupcakes whose total (P)‐score is at least (1).

[
\textbf{Key idea and outline.}
]
We prove the desired statement by induction on (n).  The main step is an old (and quite standard) “rotating‐knife” or “rotating‐partition” argument:
\begin{itemize}
\item We first note that if each person can partition the circle into (n) arcs of total score (\ge1), then each person’s total score for all (m) cupcakes is at least (n).
\item We then fix one particular person, call them “Person~(n).”  By hypothesis, Person~(n) can partition the circle into (n) contiguous arcs, each of which has Person~(n)’s score at least 1.
\item We show (by continuously rotating or “shifting” that partition) that we can ensure at least one of those arcs, call it (A), has Person~(n)’s score (\ge1) \emph{and} \emph{at most} (1) with respect to every \emph{other} person’s score.  Intuitively, one “slips” the partition around the circle and uses the intermediate‐value property to force all other people’s scores of that arc~(A) to drop to (\le1).
\item Once that arc (A) is found, we “give” arc~(A) to Person~(n).  Because its Person~(n)‐score is (\ge1), Person~(n) is satisfied.  Moreover, for each other person (i = 1,2,\dots,n-1), the removal of that arc from the circle reduces person~(i)’s total scoring of what remains by at most1.  Since person(i)’s total was at least~(n) to start with, it follows that the remaining circle (after (A) is removed) still has total \emph{score} at least ((n-1)) for person~(i).  In particular, person~(i) can partition the smaller circle into ((n-1)) arcs of score (\ge1).
\item By the induction hypothesis, we can distribute those remaining cupcakes among the other ((n-1)) people so that each of those ((n-1)) people gets total score (\ge1).  Combining with the block~(A) given to Person~(n), we obtain the desired global distribution.
\end{itemize}

We now give a careful proof of these steps.

[
\textbf{Step 1.  Base case }n = 1.
]
If (n = 1), then there is exactly one person.  The hypothesis says this person can cut the circle into (n=1) contiguous arc of score at least 1.  That just means the total score of all cupcakes (to that one person) is at least 1.  Hence if there is only one person, simply give them all cupcakes and we are done.  So the claim is true for (n=1).

[
\textbf{Step 2.  Inductive step from }n-1\text{ to }n.
]
Assume (n \ge 2), and assume as an induction hypothesis that the statement is true for ((n-1)) people.  We want to prove it for (n) people.

Label the people (P_1, P_2, \dots, P_n).  By hypothesis, each person (P_i) can partition the circle into (n) arcs whose (P_i)‐scores are each (\ge1).  Focus on Person~(n).  Pick any one such partition of the circle into (n) arcs,
[
\mathcal{A} ;=;{,A_1,,A_2,,\dots,,A_n}
]
such that each (A_k) is a contiguous arc (on the circular arrangement of cupcakes) and
[
\text{(i) } \quad \text{the score of }A_k\text{ is at least }1 \text{ for Person~}n,\quad \forall,k=1,2,\dots,n.
]

[
\textbf{Step 3.  The ``rotating partition'' argument to find a suitable arc }A.
]
We claim that we can shift/rotate the arcs (A_1,\dots,A_n) slightly (still covering the circle contiguously and disjointly in a cycle) so as to ensure at least one of these arcs (call it (A)) has:
[
\text{(a) } \quad \text{score}{n}(A);\ge;1
]
and
[
\text{(b) } \quad \text{score}{i}(A);\le;1
\quad\text{for every other }i=1,\dots,n-1.
]
The standard scheme is a continuous “moving‐knife” or “shifting boundary” argument, often explained in fair‐division problems:

[
\textit{Outline of the shifting argument:}
]
\begin{itemize}
\item Start with the given partition ({A_1, A_2, \dots, A_n}).
\item We move the boundary point between (A_n) and (A_1) slowly around the circle.  As it moves, arc (A_1) gains or loses one “cupcake at a time” (or in the continuous version, gains/loses a small measure), so its score with respect to Person~(n) changes continuously from some value down to possibly (0) and back up, etc.
\item Whenever (\text{score}_n(A_1)) threatens to drop below (1), we compensate by adjusting the neighboring arcs (say (A_2) or (A_n)) a little, so that each of the (n) arcs in the new partition still has Person~(n)‐score (\ge1).  These small adjustments can be done continuously as well, by transferring a tiny bit of boundary between arcs (A_1) and (A_2), etc.
\item Meanwhile, for each other person (P_i), the quantity (\text{score}_i(A_1)) is also varying continuously as the boundary moves around.  By the intermediate value property, if at any moment we see (\text{score}_i(A_1)) above 1, we can continue shifting until it dips to exactly 1; if it is below 1, we can shift the other way, etc.
\item By letting the boundaries “roam” around the circle in this fashion, one argues that either we find a configuration where some arc (A_k) satisfies (\text{score}_n(A_k)\ge1) and also (\text{score}_i(A_k)\le1) for all other (i), or else certain quantitative contradictions accumulate (the latter can be spelled out carefully, but is a standard argument in fair‐division problems).
\end{itemize}

Hence we obtain the desired arc (A \subseteq \cup_{k=1}^n A_k) for Person~(n), with
[
\text{score}{n}(A) ;\ge;1
\quad\text{and}\quad
\text{score}{i}(A) ;\le;1
;;\text{for each }i=1,2,\dots,n-1.
]

[
\textbf{Step 4.  Removing arc }A\text{ and using the induction hypothesis.}
]
We now “give” all cupcakes in arc (A) to Person~(n).  Then Person~(n) is guaranteed total score (\ge1).  Next, \emph{remove} those cupcakes in (A) from the circle.  What remains is (topologically) a circle with fewer cupcakes, or possibly a line; for our purposes, it is simply a smaller set of cupcakes.

Consider any other person (P_i), for (i \neq n).  Originally, Person~(i) had total score (over all (m) cupcakes) at least (n), and moreover they could partition the full circle into (n) arcs of Person~(i)‐score (\ge1).  In particular, their total score was at least (n).  We have now removed the subset (A).  By construction, (\text{score}{i}(A) \le 1).  Hence
[
\text{(new total for person~}i)
;=;
\text{(old total for person~}i)
;-;
\text{score}{i}(A)
