(\textbf{2. Why Alice’s task is so difficult: an intuitive preview.})

At first glance, one might think Alice could try to make (\mathcal{S}) very large, so that for almost every possible triangle (ABC), one can find some (R \in \mathcal{S}) making (\triangle PQR) similar.  But if (\mathcal{S}) is “too large,” then \emph{all} pairs of cities (A,B) will become connected by roads, producing a complete (or nearly complete) graph on infinitely many vertices—which cannot be embedded without edges crossing.  On the other hand, if (\mathcal{S}) is “too small,” then Bob can exploit the missing shapes to ensure that certain pairs (A,B) fail the road‐building condition.  In that case, Bob can force the resulting graph to be disconnected (at least two disconnected clusters of cities).  In either scenario, one of Alice’s winning conditions,((i),(ii)) is broken.

