[
\text{there exists some third city }C
\text{ for which none of the points }R\in \mathcal{S}
\text{ has shape parameters matching those required by }
\triangle ABC
\text{ or }
\triangle BAC.
]
Because the rule says “for \emph{every} third city (C), there \emph{is} an (R\in\mathcal{S})\dots,” a single counterexample (C) is enough to \emph{block} the road between (A,B).

