Denote these cities by (A_1,A_2,A_3,\dots.)

\medskip

\noindent
Once Bob has placed his cities, we construct roads'' between them according to the following rule.  For two distinct cities \(A\) and \(B\), we draw a road along the segment \(\overline{AB}\) if and only if \[ \text{for every third city }C\neq A,B,\text{ there is some }R\in \mathcal{S}\text{ so that } \triangle PQR\text{ is directly similar to }\triangle ABC\text{ or to }\triangle BAC. \] (Recall that (\triangle UVW) is \emph{directly similar} to (\triangle XYZ)'' means there is a composition of translations, rotations, and dilations taking (U\mapsto X,,V\mapsto Y,,W\mapsto Z); equivalently, orientation is preserved, so no reflections are allowed.)

\medskip

\noindent
\textbf{Alice's winning conditions.}
Alice \emph{wins} if both of the following hold:
[
(i)\quad \text{From any city to any other, one can travel via finitely many roads (the graph is connected),}
