\textbf{Key idea: the shape library'' of triangles from \(\mathcal{S}\).} Define  \[ \mathcal{T} \;=\;\{\triangle PQR \;|\; R\in \mathcal{S}\}, \] where each \(\triangle PQR\) is regarded \emph{up to orientation} so as to match the problem's condition (\triangle ABC) or (\triangle BAC).)  Concretely, saying
[
\triangle PQR
]
is ``directly similar to (\triangle ABC) or (\triangle BAC)'' amounts (in an orientation‐preserving sense) to saying that
(\triangle ABC) has the same sidelength ratios as some triangle in (\mathcal{T}).  In other words:

[
\begin{aligned}
&\quad \triangle ABC\text{ fails to be in the ``shape library'' }\mathcal{T}
\
