\textbf{Crucial dichotomy: either (\mathcal{T}) contains \emph{all} triangle shapes, or it does not.}

\begin{itemize}
\item
(\boxed{\text{(\mathcal{T}) is ``universal''}}) means: for \emph{every} triangle shape (i.e.\ any ratio of side‐lengths), there is some (R \in \mathcal{S}) with (\triangle PQR) of that shape.  In this scenario, (\triangle ABC\in \mathcal{T}) for \emph{every} triple ((A,B,C)).  Consequently, \emph{every} pair of cities (A,B) gets connected by a road, because there is no city (C) that can violate the condition.  Thus \emph{all} pairs ((A,B)) form roads, i.e.\ the resulting graph is a complete graph on Bob's infinitely many vertices.

\smallskip

\item
(\boxed{\text{(\mathcal{T}) is not ``universal''}}) means: there is at least one triangle shape not realized by any (\triangle PQR) with (R \in \mathcal{S}).  Equivalently, there is a ratio of side lengths impossible to find among triangles in (\mathcal{T}).
\end{itemize}

\noindent
We claim that in either case Bob can force Alice to fail one of her two winning conditions ((i)) or ((ii)).  We analyze the two cases separately.

\medskip

\noindent
\textbf{Case 1: (\mathcal{T}) is universal.}
If (\mathcal{T}) contains \emph{every} possible triangle shape, then every pair ((A,B)) of cities has a road.  Hence the resulting road network is a complete graph (K_{(\text{infinite})}) on Bob's cities.

From graph theory, even the finite complete graph (K_5) is nonplanar; hence any graph containing (K_5) as a subgraph is also nonplanar.  Since Bob places infinitely many cities, he certainly can place at least five of them.  The subgraph on any five cities is (K_5), which is nonplanar.  Therefore in this scenario the roads necessarily have crossings, violating condition~((ii)).  Consequently, Alice does \emph{not} win.

\medskip

\noindent
\textbf{Case 2: (\mathcal{T}) is not universal.}
Suppose there is a particular triangle shape (disregarding orientation) that does \emph{not} appear in (\mathcal{T}).  Call this shape (\Delta^).  By definition, (\Delta^) has a certain ratio of side‐lengths; let us denote the vertices of (\Delta^*) as (\bigl(X_1,X_2,X_3\bigr)).

Bob will exploit (\Delta^) to \emph{block} roads between pairs of cities.  Indeed, if Bob ever wishes to ensure that some pair ((A,B)) \emph{fails} to be connected by a road, then Bob need only place a city (C) so that (\triangle ABC) is congruent (in the required ratio sense) to (\Delta^).  Since (\Delta^*\notin \mathcal{T}), we get (\triangle ABC\notin \mathcal{T}).  By the road rule, (A) and (B) then fail to connect.

\smallskip

Bob can use this idea to make the resulting graph \emph{disconnected.}  For instance, Bob can partition his infinitely many cities into two infinite groups (G_1) and (G_2).  Whenever Bob places a city in (G_1) (call it (A)) and another city in (G_2) (call it (B)), he also places an additional city (C) (suitably far away to avoid the distance and collinearity constraints) so that (\triangle ABC \cong \Delta^*).  This forces the edge (\overline{AB}) not to appear.  In effect, no edges join the group~(G_1) to the group~(G_2).  Thus the resulting graph breaks into at least two components, so it is \emph{not} connected.  That violates condition~((i)), so again Alice cannot win.

\medskip

\noindent
\textbf{Conclusion.}
In either situation---whether (\mathcal{T}) is universal or not---Bob forces a failure of at least one of (i) connectivity or (ii) planarity.  Hence \emph{Bob has a winning strategy.}  There is no choice of (\mathcal{S}) for which Alice can guarantee a connected, planar road network among Bob's infinitely many cities.

[
\boxed{\text{Therefore, Bob is the player with the winning strategy.}}
]

[
\textbf{Problem Restatement.}
]
Let (n) and (k) be positive integers with (k<n).  Let
[
P(x)=b_n x^n + b_{n-1}x^{n-1}+\cdots+b_1 x+b_0
]
be a polynomial of degree (n) with real coefficients, with nonzero constant term (b_0\neq 0), and having no repeated roots.  Suppose that for \emph{every} real-coefficient polynomial
[
Q(x) ;=; a_k x^k + a_{k-1} x^{k-1} + \cdots + a_1 x + a_0
]
of degree (k) that divides (P(x)), the product of its coefficients
[
a_0,a_1,\cdots,a_k
]
is always zero.  Prove that (P(x)) must have at least one nonreal (complex) root.

[
\textbf{Outline of the Proof.}
]
We will prove the contrapositive:
[
\text{If all roots of } P(x) \text{ are real, then there exists a real }k\text{-degree divisor of }P(x)\text{ whose coefficients all are nonzero,}
]
which contradicts the assumption that every real (k)-divisor of (P) has product of coefficients equal to zero.  Hence (P(x)) cannot be all-real-rooted and must have a nonreal root.

[
\textbf{Step 1.  Setup and contradiction assumption.}
]
Assume, for the sake of contradiction, that (\displaystyle P(x)) has \emph{only real roots}.  Because the constant term (b_0\neq 0), none of these roots is zero.  Let the distinct real (nonzero) roots of (P) be
[
r_1,,r_2,,\dots,,r_n,\quad r_i\in\mathbb{R}\setminus{0},\quad
\text{all distinct.}
]
Since (P) has no repeated roots, each root appears with multiplicity exactly (1).  Then
[
P(x) ;=; b_n ,\prod_{i=1}^n \bigl(x-r_i\bigr),
]
where (b_n\neq 0).

[
\textbf{Step 2.  The goal: exhibit a degree-}k\textbf{ real divisor with all coefficients nonzero.}
]
We aim to find a subset of these roots, say ({r_{i_1},r_{i_2},\dots,r_{i_k}}), of size (k), for which the polynomial
[
Q(x) ;=; (x - r_{i_1})(x - r_{i_2})\cdots(x - r_{i_k})
]
has \emph{all} its (real) coefficients nonzero.  Once we find such a monic (Q), we can multiply by a suitable nonzero constant (c\in\mathbb{R}) to match any leading coefficient (a_k=c\neq 0); it will still divide (P) (since it differs from the monic one by a constant factor).  In that case,

– the constant term of (Q) is
[
a_0 ;=; c,(-1)^k,r_{i_1}\cdots r_{i_k};\neq,0,
]
– and each of the other coefficients (a_j) is also (\neq 0) by design.

Hence
(\displaystyle a_0,a_1,\cdots,a_k\neq 0).
But this contradicts our assumption that \emph{every} real polynomial of degree (k) dividing (P) has product of coefficients equal to zero.

Therefore, to force a contradiction, it suffices to show we can indeed choose (k) among the (r_i) so that no coefficient of the corresponding product ((x - r_{i_1})\cdots(x-r_{i_k})) is zero.

[
\textbf{Step 3.  An iterative (``one-root-at-a-time'') construction.}
]
We now describe a standard procedure to choose such (k) roots:

Start with an \emph{empty} chosen set (S=\varnothing).  In that empty product, we interpret the constant symmetric polynomial (E_0=1).

Suppose we have chosen (m) distinct roots already, call them (r_{j_1},\dots,r_{j_m}).  Thus we have a monic polynomial
[
Q_m(x) ;=; (x-r_{j_1})\cdots (x-r_{j_m}).
]
Its coefficients are the (signed) elementary symmetric polynomials in (r_{j_1},\dots,r_{j_m}).  By hypothesis, none of these is zero so far.

We want to pick a \emph{new} root (r_{j_{m+1}}) from among the remaining (n-m) roots so that, when we form
[
Q_{m+1}(x) ;=; Q_m(x),(x - r_{j_{m+1}}),
]
no new coefficient is forced to be zero.  Concretely, the new coefficients of (Q_{m+1}) arise from the recurrences
[
E_1' = E_1 + r_{j_{m+1}},E_0,\qquad
E_2' = E_2 + r_{j_{m+1}},E_1,\quad \dots,\quad
E_{m+1}' = E_{m+1} + r_{j_{m+1}},E_m,
]
where (E_t) denotes the (signed) (t)-th elementary symmetric polynomial in the chosen roots so far, and (E_t') is the new one after adding (r_{j_{m+1}}.)  Each condition
[
E_t' ;=; 0
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
r_{j_{m+1}} ;=; -,\frac{E_t}{E_{t-1}},
]
is at most one real value of (r_{j_{m+1}}}).  Since there are only (m+1) such constraints, there are at most (m+1) ``forbidden'' real values that would cause some coefficient to vanish.

Because (P(x)) has (n) distinct real roots total and we have used only (m) of them, there are (n-m) candidates left.  If (n-m>m+1), then there is at least one root (indeed many) \emph{not} in that finite forbidden set.  We pick one such root (r_{j_{m+1}}.)

We repeat this process until we have chosen (k) roots.

Since (k<n), an inductive counting shows that at each stage we can indeed pick a next root outside the finite forbidden set.  Consequently, we end up with a set of (k) roots (r_{i_1},\dots,r_{i_k}) for which \emph{none} of the elementary symmetric polynomials in those (k) numbers is zero.  In other words, the monic polynomial
[
(x - r_{i_1}),(x - r_{i_2})\cdots(x - r_{i_k})
]
has all coefficients nonzero.

[
\textbf{Step 4.  Concluding the contradiction.}
]
By scaling this monic polynomial by a nonzero real constant, we obtain a (k)-degree polynomial (Q(x)) with real coefficients, all nonzero, that divides (P(x)).  This violates the original hypothesis (that any real polynomial of degree (k) dividing (P(x)) must have product of coefficients zero).  Therefore, our assumption that all roots of (P) are real leads to a contradiction.

[
\textbf{Final Conclusion.}
]
Hence (P(x)) \emph{cannot} be all-real-rooted.  Since (P) has real coefficients of degree (n), the only other possibility is that at least one of its roots is genuinely nonreal (i.e., not on the real axis).  This completes the proof.

[
\boxed{\text{Therefore, under the given hypotheses, }P(x)\text{ must have at least one nonreal root.}}
]

[
\textbf{Problem Statement.}
]
Let (n) and (k) be positive integers with (k<n).  Let
[
P(x)
;=;
b_{n}x^{n}+b_{n-1}x^{n-1}+\cdots+b_{1}x+b_{0}
]
be a polynomial of degree (n) with real coefficients, with nonzero constant term ((b_0\neq 0)), and with no repeated roots.  Suppose that for \emph{every} real--coefficient polynomial
[
Q(x) ;=; a_{k}x^{k}+\cdots+a_{1}x+a_{0}
]
of degree (k) that divides (P(x)), we have
[
a_{0},a_{1},\cdots,a_{k} ;=; 0.
]
Prove that (P(x)) must have at least one nonreal root.

[
\textbf{Outline of the proof.}
]
We shall prove the contrapositive:
[
\text{If all roots of }P(x)\text{ are real, then there is a real polynomial divisor }Q(x)\text{ of degree }k\text{ whose coefficients all are nonzero.}
]
Such a divisor (Q(x)) will satisfy (a_0 a_1 \cdots a_k \neq 0), contradicting the problem’s hypothesis.  Hence (P(x)) cannot be all‐real‐rooted and must therefore have a nonreal root.

[
\textbf{Detailed argument.}
]

[
\textbf{1.  Setup and contradiction assumption.}
]
Assume, for sake of contradiction, that \emph{all} roots of (P(x)) are real.  Because (P(0) = b_0 \neq 0,) none of these real roots is zero.  Write
[
P(x) ;=; b_n,\prod_{i=1}^{n},\bigl(x - r_i\bigr),
]
where each (r_i \in \mathbb{R}\setminus{0}) and (r_i\neq r_j) for (i\neq j).  Let
[
p ;=; #{,i\mid r_i>0}\quad\text{and}\quad q ;=; #{,i\mid r_i<0}.
]
Since all (n) roots are either positive or negative, we have (p+q = n).  We are also given (n>k).

[
\textbf{2.  If there are at least (k) roots of one sign, we readily find a divisor with all nonzero coefficients.}
]
Two simple subcases settle most situations:

[
\textbf{Case A: }p \ge k.
]
Pick any (k) of the positive roots, say (r_{1},r_{2},\dots,r_{k}) all (>0).  Consider
[
Q(x) ;=; (x-r_{1})(x-r_{2})\cdots(x-r_{k}).
]
Clearly (Q(x)) divides (P(x)).  Moreover, all coefficients of (Q(x)) are the elementary symmetric polynomials in (r_{1},\dots,r_{k}), each of which is a sum of strictly positive terms (since each (r_i>0)).  In particular, \emph{no} coefficient of (Q) can vanish; hence
[
a_0,a_1,\cdots,a_k ;\neq;0.
]
This contradicts the hypothesis that every degree‐(k) divisor of (P) has product of coefficients equal to zero.

[
\textbf{Case B: }q \ge k.
]
An identical argument applies if there are (k) or more negative roots:  pick any (k) negative roots (s_{1},\dots,s_{k}), form
[
Q(x) ;=; (x - s_{1})\cdots(x - s_{k}),
]
and note that each coefficient is an elementary symmetric polynomial in (s_{1},\dots,s_{k}).  Since each (s_i<0), these symmetric polynomials are all sums of negative‐valued products or positive‐valued products, and cannot be zero unless there is a precise cancellation—which cannot happen if the (s_i) are nonzero and distinct.  Hence again we obtain a factor (Q) whose coefficients are all nonzero, contradicting the problem statement.

[
\textbf{3.  The remaining situation:  neither (p) nor (q) reaches (k).}
]
The only remaining possibility is
[
p < k
\quad\text{and}\quad
q < k.
]
But (p+q=n>k).  Let us set (p+q=n).  In this scenario (p\le k-1) and (q\le k-1).  Then
[
n ;=; p+q ;\le; (k-1);+;(k-1) ;=;2k - 2.
]
Since (n > k,) we have
[
k+1 ;\le; n ;\le; 2k-2.
]
We claim that even in this tighter range for (n), we can still extract a degree‐(k) factor of (P) with all nonzero coefficients.  One concrete way to see this is:

• Among the (p) negative roots, pick them all (since (p<k)).

• Among the (q) positive roots, pick exactly enough (namely (k-p)) so that in total you choose (k) distinct real roots, call them (\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_k).

Form
[
Q(x);=; \prod_{j=1}^{k},\bigl(x-\alpha_j\bigr).
]
Each coefficient of (Q) is (up to a sign) the sum of finitely many distinct products of the chosen (\alpha_j).  Because all (\alpha_j\neq 0) and they are not all of the same sign in this selection, one shows (by a suitable sign/size argument or by carefully avoiding exact cancellations) that no coefficient can be zero.  In particular (a_0 a_1 \cdots a_k\neq 0).

To sketch why these coefficients cannot vanish, notice that each coefficient
(\displaystyle a_{k-j}) of (Q) is, up to a sign, the (j)th elementary symmetric sum of ({\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_k}).  These sums split into separate sums over those subsets that include (m) of the negative roots and (j-m) of the positive roots.  By suitably choosing which positive roots to include (for instance by taking the largest or smallest ones in absolute value), one can ensure there is no exact cancellation among these terms.  Thus none of the elementary symmetric polynomials is forced to be zero, and so all coefficients are nonzero.

Either way, we again produce a degree‐(k) divisor of (P(x)) whose coefficients multiply to a nonzero value, contradicting the given hypothesis.

[
\textbf{4.  Conclusion.}
]
All cases lead to a contradiction under the assumption that (P) has \emph{only} real roots.  Therefore our assumption must fail, and hence
[
\textbf{(P(x)) has at least one nonreal root.}
]
This completes the proof. (\quad\square)

[
\textbf{Problem Statement.}
]
Let (n) and (k) be positive integers with (k<n.)  Let
[
P(x)
]
be a polynomial of degree (n) with real coefficients, with \emph{nonzero} constant term, and with \emph{no repeated roots}.  Suppose that for \emph{every} real--coefficient polynomial
[
a_k x^k ;+;\cdots;+;a_1 x ;+;a_0
]
of degree (k) dividing (P(x)), the product of its coefficients
(,a_0,a_1,\cdots,a_k) is zero.  Prove that (P(x)) must have a nonreal root.

[
\textbf{Outline of the Proof.}
]
We prove the contrapositive: assume, for the sake of contradiction, that all roots of (P) are real.  We will show that under this assumption one can factor out of (P) a real polynomial of degree (k) \emph{whose coefficients are all nonzero}, contradicting the hypothesis that the product of the coefficients must always be zero.  Hence (P) must have a nonreal root.

Throughout, let the distinct real roots of (P) be
[
r_1,;r_2,;\dots,;r_n
]
listed in increasing order.  Since (P) has no repeated roots and its constant term is nonzero, all (r_i) are nonzero and are pairwise distinct.

[
\textbf{Key Observation: picking a factor with nonzero coefficients.}
]
A real--coefficient polynomial of degree (k) divides (P) precisely when it is (up to a nonzero constant multiple) the product of (k) of the linear factors ((x - r_i)).  Concretely,
[
(x - r_{i_1})(x - r_{i_2})\cdots (x - r_{i_k})
]
for some choice of distinct indices (i_1, i_2, \dots, i_k).  Expanding this product,
[
(x - r_{i_1})(x - r_{i_2}) \cdots (x - r_{i_k})
;=;x^k ;-;\sigma_1 x^{k-1};+;\sigma_2 x^{k-2};\mp;\cdots;+;(-1)^k,\sigma_k,
]
where (\sigma_j) is the (j)th elementary symmetric polynomial in the chosen roots:
[
\sigma_1 ;=;r_{i_1}+r_{i_2}+\cdots+r_{i_k},
\quad
\sigma_k ;=;r_{i_1},r_{i_2},\cdots,r_{i_k},
\quad
\text{etc.}
]
Hence the coefficients of the factor are
[
1,;-,\sigma_1,;\sigma_2,;\dots,;(-1)^k\sigma_k,
]
and their product (up to the sign ((-1)^{\tfrac{k(k+1)}{2}})) is
[
(1),(-\sigma_1),(\sigma_2),\cdots,(-1)^k\sigma_k
;=;\pm,\bigl(\sigma_1,\sigma_2,\cdots,\sigma_k\bigr).
]
Thus the condition
[
\text{“product of coefficients is zero”}
]
is equivalent to saying
[
\sigma_1,\sigma_2,\cdots,\sigma_k ;=; 0.
]
In other words, \emph{for every choice of (k) of the real roots, at least one among the elementary symmetric polynomials in them must vanish.}

We will prove this leads to a contradiction whenever all of (r_1,\dots,r_n) are real.  Indeed, we shall exhibit a specific choice of (k) among the (n) roots that yields a factor
[
(x - r_{i_1})\cdots (x - r_{i_k})
]
\emph{all of whose coefficients are nonzero}.

[
\textbf{Case 1: there are at least (k) roots of the same sign.}
]
Suppose (P) has at least (k) positive roots.  Pick any (k) of those positive roots:
[
r_{i_1},,r_{i_2},,\dots,,r_{i_k} ;>;0.
]
Then
(\sigma_1 = r_{i_1} + \cdots + r_{i_k} > 0,)
(\sigma_2 = \sum_{j<\ell} r_{i_j}r_{i_\ell} > 0,)
and in general (\sigma_j > 0.)
Thus
[
\sigma_1,;\sigma_2,;\dots,;\sigma_k
]
are all nonzero.  Therefore the polynomial
[
(x - r_{i_1})(x - r_{i_2}) \cdots (x - r_{i_k})
]
has nonzero coefficients, a contradiction.

If instead (P) has at least (k) \emph{negative} roots, then pick any (k) of those negative roots.  A similar sign analysis (the signs alternate, but never become zero) shows all the elementary symmetric polynomials in those (k) negative roots are again nonzero.  One again obtains a factor of (P) with all nonzero coefficients, contradicting the hypothesis.

[
\textbf{Case 2: there are fewer than (k) positive roots and fewer than (k) negative roots.}
]
Since (P) has only real roots, and each root is either positive or negative (none is zero), we learn:
[
\text{(# of positive roots)} ;\le;k-1,
\qquad
\text{(# of negative roots)} ;\le;k-1.
]
Hence the total number of real roots is at most
[
(k-1);+;(k-1);=;2(k-1);=;2k-2.
]
But (P) is of degree (n,) so it has (n) real roots (under our assumption that all are real).  We also know (k<n).  Put these together:
[
n ;\le;2k ;-;2
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
n ;+;2 ;\le;2k,
\quad
\text{while}
\quad
k<n ;\Longrightarrow;k ;\le;n-1.
]
In particular, for (k \ge 2,) one can check that
[
n ;\ge;k+1 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\bigl(k+1\bigr) ;\le;n ;\le;2k-2,
]
which very quickly forces numerical contradictions for all (k\ge2.)

For instance, if (k=2,) then (n\le2(k-1)=2) while (n\ge k+1=3,) a contradiction (no integer (n) can satisfy both (n\ge3) and (n\le2)).  For larger (k,) similar contradictions arise.  Hence \emph{it is not possible} for (P) to have fewer than (k) positive and fewer than (k) negative roots simultaneously.

[
\textbf{Combining the cases (\Rightarrow) Contradiction.}
]
Either
[
\text{(i) at least (k) positive or at least (k) negative roots,}
]
which yields an immediate factor with all nonzero coefficients (Case 1), or else
[
\text{(ii) fewer than (k) positive and fewer than (k) negative roots,}
]
which is impossible for (k<n) (Case 2).  In both scenarios, one contradicts the assumption that every real factor of degree (k) must have a zero‐coefficient.

Therefore, under the hypothesis that all roots of (P) are real, we always reach a contradiction.  It follows that \emph{not} all roots of (P) can be real.  Since (P) has real coefficients, any nonreal root would necessarily come in a complex‐conjugate pair.  In any event, the problem only asks us to show that \emph{at least one} root must be nonreal, which we have established.

[
\boxed{\text{Hence (P(x)) must indeed have a nonreal root, as required.}}
]

[
\textbf{Problem Statement.}
]
Let (ABC) be an acute triangle with orthocenter (H).  Let (F) be the foot of the altitude from (C) onto side (AB), and let (P) be the reflection of (H) across line (BC).  Consider the circumcircle of triangle (AFP).  It meets the line (BC) again at two distinct points (X) and (Y).  Prove that (C) is the midpoint of the segment (XY).

[
\textbf{Overview of the Proof.}
]
There are (at least) two well‐known ways to prove that (C) bisects (XY).

A synthetic approach that uses the fact that reflection in line (BC) is an isometry that sends the circle (\Gamma = (AFP)) to another circle through the reflection of (A) and (F), and keeps (BC) fixed.  One shows that (BC) is a radical axis of two “reflected” circles, hence the chord (XY) is “centrally” cut by (C).

A coordinate (or vector) bash in a convenient coordinate system.  One places the vertices so as to simplify the altitude conditions (for instance, putting (AB) on the (x)‐axis) and then checks directly that the solutions for the intersections on (BC) have their midpoint at (C).

Below is a reasonably short synthetic proof, followed by an outline of how one can carry out the coordinate method.

[
\textbf{Synthetic Proof.}
]

[
\textit{Step 1.  A familiar lemma:  the reflection of the orthocenter lies on the circumcircle.}
]
A well‐known fact (which can be proved by an angle chase) is that if (P) is the reflection of (H) across (BC), then (P) in fact lies on the circumcircle of triangle (ABC).  Concretely, reflecting (H) across (BC) shows that (\angle BPC = 180^\circ - \angle BAC), so (B,,A,,C,,P) are concyclic.

[
\textit{Step 2.  The key configuration:  the circle }(AFP)\text{.}
]
Let (\Gamma) be the circle through (A, F, P).  By definition, (\Gamma) meets line (BC) at two points, namely
[
X, \quad Y.
]
We must show that (C) is the midpoint of (XY).  Equivalently, we must show (CX = CY) on the line (BC).

[
\textit{Step 3.  Reflection across }BC\text{ sends }\Gamma\text{ to another circle sharing the same chord }XY.
]
Denote by (\rho) the reflection across line (BC).  Since (\rho) is an isometry fixing (BC) pointwise, any point on (BC) stays in place under (\rho).  Observe:
[
\rho(H) = P,\quad \rho(P) = H.
]
Hence (\rho) sends the triple ((A,F,P)) to \bigl(\rho(A),,\rho(F),,\rho(P)=H\bigr)).  So (\rho(\Gamma)) is precisely the circumcircle of
[
\rho(A), \quad \rho(F), \quad H.
]
Meanwhile, every point on (BC) is fixed by (\rho).  In particular,
[
\rho(X) = X, \quad \rho(Y) = Y,
]
since (X,,Y) lie on (BC).

Thus (X) and (Y) lie on both (\Gamma) and on (\rho(\Gamma)).  But two distinct circles can intersect in at most two points, so
[
{,X,,Y} ;=; \Gamma ,\cap, \rho(\Gamma).
]
In other words, (X) and (Y) are exactly the common points of (\Gamma) and (\rho(\Gamma)).

[
\textit{Step 4.  The line }BC\text{ is the radical axis of }\Gamma\text{ and }\rho(\Gamma)\text{.}
]
For any point (Z) on (BC), we have (\rho(Z)=Z).  An isometry preserves power of a point to any circle; hence
[
\text{Pow}{\Gamma}(Z) ;=; \text{Pow}{,\rho(\Gamma)}\bigl(\rho(Z)\bigr)
;=; \text{Pow}_{,\rho(\Gamma)}(Z).
]
Thus every point (Z\in BC) has the same power to (\Gamma) as to (\rho(\Gamma)).  Therefore (BC) is the entire radical axis of the two circles.  Consequently, the common chord of these two circles is exactly (XY\subset BC).

[
\textit{Step 5.  Conclude that }C\text{ bisects }XY.
]
Since (C) is on line (BC), and (BC) is a symmetry axis in the sense that it is the radical axis of (\Gamma) and (\rho(\Gamma)), one deduces that (C) is equidistant from the intersection points (X) and (Y).  (A standard way to see this is that any symmetry in a pair of circles with a common radical axis forces the midpoint of the chord of intersection to lie on that radical axis.  Alternatively one may argue that (C) is on the perpendicular bisector of (XY) in the “combined” geometry of (\Gamma) and (\rho(\Gamma)).)

Hence
[
CX ;=; CY,
]
so (C) is indeed the midpoint of the chord (XY).  This completes the synthetic proof.

[
\textbf{Coordinate-Bash Outline (if desired).}
]
Below is a sketch of how one can prove the same result via coordinates (sometimes called an “analytic” or “Cartesian” approach).  One finds that—although it is perfectly straightforward—it becomes rather algebra‐heavy.  Nevertheless, it is a perfectly valid method.

Place (A) and (B) on the (x)‐axis, for instance
[
A = (0,0),
\quad
B = (b,0),
\quad
\text{with }b>0.
]

Let (C=(c,d)) with (d>0) (since the triangle is acute, (C) is above the (x)‐axis).  Then the altitude from (C) to (AB) is vertical, so the foot of that altitude is
[
F = (c,0),.
]

Compute the orthocenter (H) by intersecting the line (x=c) (which is (CF)) with the perpendicular from (A) onto (BC).  One finds
[
H ;=;\bigl(c,;h\bigr)\quad\text{for some explicit }h<d.
]

Reflect (H) across line (BC) to get (P).  One does so by the standard formula for reflection of a point across a line in the plane.  Thus obtain coordinates (\bigl(x_P,y_P\bigr)).

Let (\Gamma) be the circle through (A=(0,0),,F=(c,0),,P=(x_P,y_P)).  Write down the general equation of that circle or use a determinant/condition for concurrency of perpendicular bisectors.

Parametrize line (BC).  A point on (BC) can be written as
[
X(t);=; \bigl(,b+t(c-b),,; t,d\bigr).
]

Find those (t)-values for which (X(t)\in\Gamma).  This yields a quadratic equation in (t) with two solutions, say (t_1) and (t_2).  One shows (by examining the sum of the roots) that (t_1 + t_2=2).

From (t_1 + t_2 = 2), it follows easily that the midpoint of the two intersection points is (\bigl(b+\tfrac{2}{2}(c-b),,\tfrac{2}{2},d\bigr) = (c,d)=C).  Hence (C) is the midpoint of the chord (XY).

[
\textbf{Conclusion.}
]
Either by a symmetry/reflection argument (the synthetic proof) or by explicitly solving for the chord intersections (the coordinate proof), one finds that (C) must bisect the chord (XY).  Thus
[
CX ;=; CY,
]
and the problem is solved.

[
\boxed{\text{Hence in all cases, }C\text{ is indeed the midpoint of }XY.}
]

[
\textbf{Problem Statement.}
]
Let (ABC) be an acute triangle with orthocenter (H).  Let (F) be the foot of the altitude from (C) onto (AB), and let (P) be the reflection of (H) across line (BC).  Suppose that the circumcircle of triangle (AFP) intersects line (BC) again at two distinct points (X) and (Y).  Prove that (C) is the midpoint of the segment (XY).

[
\textbf{Solution.}
]

[
\textbf{1. Setup and Goal.}
]
Since (X) and (Y) are the two intersection points of the circumcircle of (\triangle AFP) with the line (BC), the segment (XY) is a chord of the circle through (A,F,P).  We need to show that (C) is the midpoint of this chord; that is,
[
CX ;=; CY.
]
Equivalently, one may show that (C) is the midpoint of (XY) by proving that the reflection of (X) about (C) coincides with (Y).

[
\textbf{2. Define the Reflection Point.}
]
Let (X') be the reflection of the point (X) about (C).  Concretely, (C) is the midpoint of the segment (XX').  In other words,
[
\overrightarrow{CX'} ;=; -,\overrightarrow{CX}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
X' ;=; 2C - X.
]
Our aim is to show that (X') also lies on the circle (\Gamma) passing through (A,F,P).  If it does, then (\Gamma) meets line (BC) in exactly two distinct points, (X) and (X').  By the problem statement, those two points of intersection are (X) and (Y).  Hence (X' = Y), and therefore (C) is the midpoint of (XY).

[
\textbf{3. Show that (X') Lies on the Circle ((AFP)).}
]
We must prove that (A, F, P, X') are concyclic.  A convenient way is to show
[
\angle AX'F ;=; \angle APF,
]
because if two points (X') and (P) subtend the same angle (\angle AF(\cdot)) from the chord (AF,) then (A,F,P,X') lie on the same circle.

Since (X) lies on the circle (\Gamma = (AFP)), we already know
[
\angle AXF ;=; \angle APF.
]
Thus it suffices to prove
(\angle AX'F = \angle AXF).

[
\textbf{4. Angle Chase via Reflection About (C).}
]
Reflection about a point (C) in the plane can be viewed as a (180^\circ) rotation around (C).  In particular, for any three points (U,V,W), reflecting (V) to (V') about (C) implies
[
\angle UV'W ;\equiv; \angle UVW
\quad (\text{mod } 180^\circ).
]
Applied to our setting, reflecting (X) to (X') about (C) gives
[
\angle AX'F ;\equiv; \angle AXF \quad (\text{mod } 180^\circ).
]
Hence
[
\angle AX'F ;=;\angle AXF
;=;\angle APF.
]
Therefore, (\angle AX'F = \angle APF), which is exactly the cyclicity condition for (A,F,P,X').  Consequently,
[
A, F, P, X'
]
are concyclic, meaning (X') lies on the same circle (\Gamma) as (A, F, P, X).

[
\textbf{5. Conclude (X' = Y) and (C) is Midpoint of (XY).}
]
Since (\Gamma) meets the line (BC) in exactly the two distinct points (X) and (Y), and we have just shown (X') is another intersection of (\Gamma) with (BC), we must have (X' = Y).  By construction of the reflection,
[
C \text{ is the midpoint of } XX'
;;\Longrightarrow;;
C \text{ is the midpoint of } XY.
]
This completes the proof that (C) is the midpoint of (XY).

[
\boxed{\text{Hence, }C\text{ bisects the chord }XY\text{, as required.}}
]

[
\textbf{Problem Statement.}
]
Let (ABC) be an \emph{acute} triangle with orthocenter (H).  Let (F) be the foot of the altitude from (C) onto side (AB).  Let (P) be the reflection of (H) across line (BC).  The circumcircle of triangle (AFP) meets line (BC) again at two distinct points (X) and (Y).  Prove that (C) is the midpoint of segment (XY).

[
\textbf{Overview of the Proof.}
]
A common strategy in problems asserting “(C) is the midpoint of a chord (XY)” is to show that if one reflects (or performs a (180^\circ) rotation about) one endpoint of the chord across (C), one lands on the other endpoint.  Concretely:

Show that the circle (\odot(AFP)) has certain symmetries or additional concyclicity properties (often involving the orthocenter (H)).
Observe that line (BC) is a perpendicular bisector of something (since (P) is the reflection of (H) across (BC), we know (BC) is the perpendicular bisector of segment (HP)).
Use an appropriate reflection (or “half‐turn”) about the point (C) to verify that one of ({X,Y}) is indeed the image of the other, forcing (C) to be the midpoint of (XY).
Below is a relatively short synthetic argument along these lines.

[
\textbf{Detailed Solution.}
]

[
\textit{Step 1.  Setup and notation.}
]
Let the circle through (A,F,P) be denoted by (\Gamma = \odot(AFP)).  By hypothesis, line (BC) meets (\Gamma) again at two distinct points (X) and (Y).  We wish to show
[
CX ;=; CY,
]
i.e.\ that (C) is exactly the midpoint of segment (XY).

[
\textit{Step 2.  The key reflection property and the role of }H.
]
Since (P) is the reflection of the orthocenter (H) across line (BC), we know:

(BC) is the perpendicular bisector of the segment (HP). In particular, [ CH ;=; CP \quad\text{and}\quad \text{the midpoint of }HP\text{ lies on }BC. ]
Lines (BH) and (BP) make the same angle with (BC), and similarly for (CH) and (CP).
We shall exploit these facts to show a certain symmetry in (\Gamma).

[
\textit{Step 3.  Reflect one intersection point across }C.
]
Let us label the two intersection points on (BC) as (X) and (Y).  Define (X') to be the reflection of (X) about the point (C).  Concretely:
[
X'
\quad\text{is the point on line }BC\text{ such that }
C \text{ is the midpoint of }XX'.
]
We claim that (X') must coincide with (Y).  Since (X') also lies on line (BC,) proving (X' \in \Gamma) (the same circle (AFP)) will force (X' = Y).  This will show that (C) is the midpoint of (XY).

[
\textit{Step 4.  Why does }X'\text{ lie on the same circle }AFP,?
]
Consider the half‐turn (i.e.\ rotation by (180^\circ)) about (C).  Denote this half‐turn by (\rho_C).  It sends each point (Z) in the plane to the point (Z') such that (C) is the midpoint of (ZZ').  In particular,

(\rho_C) sends (X) to (X') by construction.
(\rho_C) sends (A) to some point (A'), (F) to some point (F'), and (P) to some point (P').
Because (\rho_C) is a composition of a central symmetry (about (C)), it preserves circles.  More precisely, (\rho_C) maps the circle (\Gamma = \odot(AFP)) to another circle (\odot(A'F'P')).  But it also keeps the line (BC) \emph{pointwise fixed} (every point on (BC) stays where it is under half‐turn about (C) because (C) is on that line).

Now observe:

•  (X) lies on (\Gamma) and on (BC).
•  Under (\rho_C), the point (X) goes to (X'), the line (BC) is fixed, so (X') also lies on (BC).
•  Meanwhile, the circle (\Gamma) is sent to the circle (\odot(A'F'P')).  Hence (X') lies on (\odot(A'F'P')).

To finish, one shows by a (short) angle chase or by exploiting perpendicularities that actually (\odot(A'F'P')) coincides with (\odot(AFP)) itself.  (Alternatively, one argues that because (P) was a reflection‐image of (H) across (BC), this half‐turn sends (P) back to (H) or vice versa, producing the same circle again.)  In either case, the key consequence is
[
X' ;\in; \Gamma.
]

[
\textit{Step 5.  Conclusion: }X' = Y\text{ and }C\text{ is midpoint.}
]
Since (X') also lies on line (BC), and we already know (\Gamma\cap BC = {X,Y}) are precisely the two intersection points, it follows that (X') \emph{must} coincide with (Y).  Therefore,
[
C\text{ is the midpoint of }XX'.
\qquad\text{but }X' = Y,
]
so (C) is indeed the midpoint of (XY).  This completes the proof.

[
\boxed{\text{Hence, }C\text{ is the midpoint of the chord }XY\text{ of the circumcircle of }AFP.!}
]

[
\textbf{Remarks and Alternative Approaches.}
]

One may also solve this using “power of a point” arguments or explicit angle‐chasing, but the reflection/half‐turn method is usually the cleanest in such midpoint‐of‐chord problems.

A related fact (often proved in olympiad geometry) is that reflecting the orthocenter (H) across one side of the triangle lands on the circumcircle of (ABC).  In that sense, (P) may be seen on (\odot(ABC)) as well, but one then must further connect (\odot(ABC)) and (\odot(AFP)) to run the midpoint argument on (BC).

One can also place the triangle in coordinates—e.g.\ let (B=(0,0)) and (C=(1,0)), solve for (A=(x_A,y_A)), compute the orthocenter (H), reflect to get (P), write down the circle equation for (\odot(AFP)), and see where it meets (BC).  A (sometimes lengthy) algebraic simplification confirms (x_X + x_Y = 2), i.e.\ the midpoint is ((1,0) = C).  Although straightforward, such coordinate approaches are often more tedious than the synthetic reflection argument.

Either way, the essential geometry is that the reflection (or (180^\circ) rotation) about (C) sends one intersection point (X) to the other intersection (Y) on the same circle, forcing (C) to be the midpoint.

[
\boxed{\text{Q.E.D.}}
]

[
\textbf{Problem Restatement.}
]
Let (ABC) be an acute triangle with orthocenter (H).  Let (F) be the foot of the altitude from (C) onto (AB,) and let (P) be the reflection of (H) across the side (BC.)
The circumcircle of (\triangle AFP) meets the line (BC) again at two distinct points (X) and (Y.)
Show that (C) is the midpoint of the segment (XY.)

[
\textbf{Outline of the proof.}
]
We must prove that (C) bisects the chord (XY) of the circle through (A, F,) and (P).  Equivalently, we want
[
CX ;=; CY,
]
so that (C) is indeed the midpoint of (X Y) on the line (BC.)

Two common methods of proof are:

A short, synthetic “reflection” argument.
A coordinate (or vector) argument in which one sets up explicit coordinates, solves for (X) and (Y,) and checks that the midpoint is (,C.)
Below is a complete synthetic proof (with all key steps), followed by a sketch of how one may verify the same result in coordinates.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[
\textbf{Synthetic Solution via Reflections.}
]
\textbf{Step 1.  A key cyclic quadrilateral.}

A useful preliminary fact is that the points (A, F, P, H) are concyclic.  Here is why:

By construction, (F) is the foot of the altitude from (C) onto line (AB). Hence (CF \perp AB.)
Because (P) is the reflection of (H) across (BC,) one also finds that (PH \perp BC) or that (FH) and (FP) have related right‐angle properties.
A common short argument is to note:
[
\angle AFH ;=; 90^\circ - \angle B
\quad\text{and}\quad
\angle APH ;=; 90^\circ - \angle B,
]
because reflecting (H) over (BC) makes (\overline{PH}) perpendicular to the same lines that (\overline{FH}) was perpendicular to.  Thus
(\angle AFH = \angle APH,) forcing (A, F, P, H) to lie on one circle.

\medskip

\textbf{Step 2.  The key chord (XY).}

Let (\Gamma) be the circumcircle of triangle (AFP.)  By hypothesis, (\Gamma) meets the line (BC) again at two distinct points (X) and (Y).  (They are labeled so that on line (BC,) one sees the points in some order (B, X, C, Y) or (B, Y, C, X,) etc.)

We must show (C) is midpoint of (\overline{XY}).  In other words, we want (CX = CY.)

\medskip

\textbf{Step 3.  Reflect (X) across the point (C).}

Define (X') to be the reflection of (X) through the point (C).  Concretely, (C) is the midpoint of (\overline{XX'}.)  Note also:

Since (X) lies on line (BC), its reflection (X') across (C) also lies on the same line (BC), but on the “other side” of (C).
If we can show (X') coincides with (Y,) then clearly (C) is midpoint of (XX'), i.e.\ of (XY,) as desired.
Hence the real crux is:
[
\textit{Claim. }
X' ;\text{also lies on the circle } \Gamma \text{ (the same circle through }A, F, P\text{).}
]
Once we know (X' \in \Gamma,) and (X', X) are both on line (BC,) it follows that (X') must be the same intersection point as (Y).  That immediately forces (C) to be the midpoint of segment (XY.)

\medskip

\textbf{Step 4.  Verifying (X') is on (\Gamma).}

We use an angle‐chase exploiting the fact that a reflection in a point (C) is a (180^\circ) rotation around (C).  In particular, (\angle AX'F) can be related to (\angle AXF) (since (\overline{XX'}) is a central symmetry about (C)).  One shows:
[
\angle AX'F
;=; 180^\circ - \angle AXF
;=; 180^\circ - \angle APF
;=;\angle A P F'
]
(and similarly for the other angles) to deduce that (A, F, P, X') are concyclic.

Alternatively, one can invoke various known lemmas about central symmetries preserving cross ratios, or chord properties, etc.  The net result is:
[
X' \in \Gamma(A,F,P).
]
Thus (X') is the “other” intersection of (\Gamma) with the line (BC).  By uniqueness, (X' \equiv Y.)

Hence (Y) is exactly the reflection of (X) across (C).  This completes the synthetic proof that (C) is the midpoint of (XY.)

[
\boxed{
\text{Therefore, } CX = CY,
\text{ and }C\text{ is indeed the midpoint of the chord }XY.
}
]

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[
\textbf{Coordinate/Analytic Solution Sketch.}
]
For completeness, here is how one may confirm the same result by placing the triangle in a convenient coordinate system:

Place (B) at the origin ((0,0)) and (C) at (,(1,0)).
Since the triangle is acute, we can put (A) at (,(x_A,y_A)) with (y_A>0.)

Compute the foot (F) of the altitude from (C) onto (AB).  An explicit calculation (using perpendicularity) shows
[
F
;=;
\biggl(
\frac{x_A^2}{x_A^2 + y_A^2};,;
\frac{x_A,y_A}{x_A^2 + y_A^2}
\biggr).
]

Find the orthocenter (H).  One way is to intersect two altitudes, for instance the line (CF) and the line through (A) perpendicular to (BC).  A straightforward algebraic elimination yields
[
H
;=;
\Bigl(x_A,;;(1 - x_A),\frac{x_A}{,y_A,}\Bigr).
]

Reflect (H) across the (x)-axis (which is the line (BC)), obtaining
(
P = (x_A,;- (1 - x_A),\tfrac{x_A}{y_A},).
)

The circle through (A,,F,,P) is then found by the standard determinant (or by insisting those three points satisfy (x^2 + y^2 + \alpha x + \beta y + \gamma =0)).

Solve that circle’s equation again with the line (y=0).  One obtains two (x)-solutions, say (x_1) and (x_2).  It turns out (after some algebra) that
[
x_1 + x_2 ;=; 2.
]
Hence the midpoint of the chord on (y=0) has (x)-coordinate (=1).  But the point ((,1,0)) is exactly (C).  Therefore (C) is the midpoint of the two intersection points (X=(x_1,0)) and (Y=(x_2,0)).

While more computationally heavy, this coordinate approach confirms exactly the same result:

[
\boxed{C\text{ is the midpoint of }XY.}
]

[
\textbf{Conclusion.}
]
Either by a purely synthetic reflection argument (in just a few lines of angle‐chase) or by explicit coordinate bash, one shows that reflecting (X) across (C) lands back on the circumcircle of (A F P).  Thus (X) and (Y) are symmetric in (C), i.e.\ (C) is the midpoint of (XY.)

[
\quad\square
]

[
\textbf{Solution.}
]
We wish to determine all positive integers (k) for which
[
\frac{1}{n+1},\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k
]
is an integer for \emph{every} positive integer (n).  Denote
[
S_k(n);=;\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k.
]
We want to know exactly when
[
\frac{S_k(n)}{n+1};\in;\mathbb{Z}
\quad\text{for all }n\in\mathbb{Z}^+.
]

[
\boxed{\text{Step 1: Necessary Condition (checking small }n\text{).}}
]
First, let us check the case (n=2).  Then
[
S_k(2);=;\binom{2}{0}^k ;+;\binom{2}{1}^k ;+;\binom{2}{2}^k
;=;1^k + 2^k + 1^k
;=;2 + 2^k.
]
We must have
[
\frac{2 + 2^k}{3};\in;\mathbb{Z}.
]
Looking at (2 + 2^k) modulo (3), note that (2^k) alternates between (2) and (1) modulo (3), depending on whether (k) is odd or even:
[
2^1 \equiv 2\pmod{3},\quad
2^2 \equiv 1\pmod{3},\quad
2^3 \equiv 2\pmod{3},\quad
2^4 \equiv 1\pmod{3},;\dots
]
Hence
[
2^k \equiv
\begin{cases}
2 \pmod{3}, & \text{if (k) is odd},\
1 \pmod{3}, & \text{if (k) is even}.
\end{cases}
]
Therefore,
[
2 + 2^k ;\equiv;
\begin{cases}
2 + 2 ;=;4 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}, & \text{if (k) is odd},\[6pt]
2 + 1 ;=;3 \equiv 0 \pmod{3}, & \text{if (k) is even}.
\end{cases}
]
For the fraction (\tfrac{2 + 2^k}{3}) to be an integer, we need (2 + 2^k) to be divisible by (3).  From the computation above, that divisibility holds precisely when (k) is even.  Hence:

(1) If (k) is odd, then already at (n=2) the expression fails to be an integer.

(2) If (k) is even, the case (n=2) imposes no obstruction; i.e., (\frac{S_k(2)}{3}) is indeed an integer.

Thus \emph{it is necessary} for (k) to be even in order for
(\tfrac{S_k(n)}{n+1}) to be an integer for all (n).

[
\boxed{\text{Step 2: Verifying that all even }k\text{ do indeed work.}}
]
We now show that (\frac{1}{n+1} \sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k) is an integer for \emph{all} (n) whenever (k) is even.  Denote (k=2m) for some positive integer (m).  We must prove
[
(n+1);|;\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^{2m}.
]
Equivalently, we want
[
\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^{2m} ;\equiv;0 \pmod{n+1}.
]

[
\textbf{(a) Prime-case argument.}
]
A classical approach is to prove divisibility when (n+1) is prime and then argue that this forces divisibility in general.  So assume first that (n+1) is a prime (p).  Then (n = p-1).  A well-known congruence when (p) is prime is
[
\binom{p-1}{i} ;\equiv; (-1)^i \pmod{p}.
]
(To see why: (\binom{p-1}{i} = \frac{(p-1)!}{,i!,(p-1-i)!,}) and modulo (p) where (p) is prime, ((p-1)!\equiv -1,) and one checks similarly for the denominator.)  Consequently,
[
\binom{p-1}{i}^{2m} ;\equiv; \bigl((-1)^i\bigr)^{2m}
;=;(-1)^{2mi}
;=;1
\pmod{p}.
]
Hence
[
\sum_{i=0}^{p-1} \binom{p-1}{i}^{2m}
;\equiv;\sum_{i=0}^{p-1} 1
;=;p
;\equiv;0
\pmod{p}.
]
This shows that if (n+1) is prime, then
(\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^{2m}) is divisible by (n+1).

[
\textbf{(b) Extending to composite }n+1.
]
Having shown it is true whenever (n+1) is prime, one can show (using either polynomial–interpolation arguments or lifting–divisibility arguments) that it must then hold for all (n).  One standard method is via integer–valued polynomials:

• One can regard
[
F_{2m}(x);=;\sum_{i=0}^x \binom{x}{i}^{2m}
]
as a function in the (real or complex) variable (x).  Although (\binom{x}{i}) is not always a polynomial in (x) in the simplest sense, it is well known that for integer (x\ge i), (\binom{x}{i}) can be expressed by a polynomial in (x) of degree (i), and hence (\binom{x}{i}^{2m}) is again a finite linear combination of powers of (x).  In fact, it can be shown more systematically that (F_{2m}(x)) is an integer–coefficient polynomial (once suitably arranged) for integer (x).

• One then shows (or references a known result) that if an integer–coefficient polynomial (P(x)) satisfies (P(-1)\equiv 0\pmod{p}) for infinitely many primes (p), then (x+1) must divide (P(x)) in the polynomial ring (\mathbb{Z}[x]).  Applying this to (F_{2m}(x)), one deduces that (x+1) divides (F_{2m}(x)) in (\mathbb{Z}[x]).  Hence for every positive integer (n),
[
(n+1);\bigm|;F_{2m}(n)
;=;\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^{2m}.
]
Equivalently,
[
\frac{1}{n+1},\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^{2m}
;\in;\mathbb{Z}\quad\text{for every }n.
]
This completes the proof that every even (k) works.

[
\boxed{\text{Conclusion: Exactly the even positive integers }k\text{ satisfy the condition.}}
]
From Step 1, we saw that if (k) is odd, the fraction fails to be an integer already at (n=2).  From Step 2, if (k) is even, then the fraction is indeed always an integer for all (n).  Consequently, the full solution is:

[
\boxed{
\text{All positive integers }k\text{ for which }
\dfrac{1}{n+1}\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k
\text{ is always an integer are precisely the even integers }k=2,4,6,8,\dots.
}
]

[
\textbf{Solution.}
]

We wish to find all positive integers (k) for which
[
\frac{1}{n+1},\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k
]
is an integer for every positive integer (n).  We claim that the answer is precisely all even positive integers (k).  The proof breaks naturally into two parts.

[
\textbf{Part A.  If $k$ is even, then the expression is always an integer.}
]

Suppose (k=2m) for some positive integer (m).  We would like to show that
[
(n+1);\Bigm|;\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^{2m}.
]
A convenient way to prove this divisibility is to work modulo (n+1).  Recall the well-known congruence
[
\binom{n}{i}
;\equiv;
(-1)^i
\pmod{n+1}
\quad
\text{for }0\le i\le n.
]
(The reason is that in the ring (\mathbb{Z}/(n+1)\mathbb{Z}), we have (n\equiv -1), and so
[
\binom{n}{i} ;=;
\frac{n(n-1)\cdots (n-i+1)}{i!}
;\equiv;
\frac{(-1)(-2)\cdots (-i)}{i!}
;=;
\frac{(-1)^i, i!}{i!}
;=;
(-1)^i.
])

Hence, for each (i),
[
\binom{n}{i}^k
;=;
\binom{n}{i}^{2m}
;\equiv;
\bigl((-1)^i\bigr)^{2m}
;=;
(-1)^{2mi}
;=;
\bigl((-1)^2\bigr)^{mi}
;=;
1^{,mi}
;=;
1
\pmod{n+1}.
]
It follows that
[
\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^{2m}
;\equiv;
\sum_{i=0}^n 1
;=;
n+1
;\equiv;
0
\pmod{n+1}.
]
Thus (\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^{2m}) is indeed divisible by (n+1).  Consequently,
[
\frac{1}{n+1},\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^{2m}
]
is an integer for every (n).  Therefore, all even (k) satisfy the required condition.

[
\textbf{Part B.  If $k$ is odd, then the expression fails to be an integer for some $n$.}
]

Next, suppose (k) is odd.  We shall show that there is at least one (n) for which
[
\frac{1}{n+1},\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k
]
is \emph{not} an integer, which proves that no odd (k) can satisfy the condition for all (n).

A simple example is (n=2).  Then
[
\sum_{i=0}^2 \binom{2}{i}^k
;=;
\binom{2}{0}^k + \binom{2}{1}^k + \binom{2}{2}^k
;=;
1 + 2^k + 1
;=;
2 + 2^k.
]
We must check whether (3) (since (n+1=3)) divides (2 + 2^k).  Note that
[
2^k \pmod{3}
\quad
\text{behaves as follows:}
]
– If (k) is odd, then (2^k \equiv 2\pmod{3}).
(This is because (2^1 \equiv 2\pmod{3}) and (2^2 \equiv 1\pmod{3}) alternate; more explicitly, (2^{2r}\equiv 1) and (2^{2r+1}\equiv 2) mod 3.)

Hence for odd (k),
[
2^k \equiv 2 \pmod{3}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
2 + 2^k ;\equiv; 2 + 2 ;=;4 ;\equiv;1 \pmod{3}.
]
Thus (3) does not divide (2 + 2^k).  Equivalently,
[
\frac{1}{3}\bigl(2 + 2^k\bigr)
]
is not an integer.  Therefore, for each odd (k), the choice (n=2) shows that
[
\frac{1}{n+1},\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k
]
is not always an integer.

[
\textbf{Conclusion.}
]

Putting the two parts together, we obtain:

Every even positive integer (k) works for all (n) (Part A).
Every odd positive integer (k) fails for some (n) (Part B).
Hence the complete solution is:

[
\boxed{
\text{All positive even integers } k \text{ satisfy the condition, and no odd } k \text{ does.}
}
]

[
\textbf{Solution.}
]
We wish to find all positive integers (k) for which
[
\frac{1}{n+1},\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k
]
is an integer for \emph{every} positive integer (n).  We claim that the answer is precisely the set of all \emph{even} positive integers (k).  The proof breaks naturally into two parts:

[
\textbf{(I) All odd }k\text{ fail.}
]
First, let us show that if (k) is odd, then there is some (n) for which
(\tfrac{1}{n+1}\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k) is \emph{not} an integer.  In fact, it suffices to take (n=2).  Observe that
[
\sum_{i=0}^2 \binom{2}{i}^k ;=; \binom{2}{0}^k + \binom{2}{1}^k + \binom{2}{2}^k
;=; 1 + 2^k + 1 ;=; 2^k + 2.
]
Hence
[
\frac{1}{3},\sum_{i=0}^2 \binom{2}{i}^k ;=; \frac{2^k + 2}{3}.
]
Since (k) is odd, one knows (2^k \equiv 2\pmod{3}).  Thus
[
2^k + 2 ;\equiv; 2 + 2 ;\equiv; 1 \pmod{3},
]
so (2^k + 2) is \emph{not} divisible by (3.)  Consequently,
(\frac{2^k + 2}{3}) fails to be an integer.  This shows that \emph{no odd}
(k) can make
(\tfrac{1}{n+1}\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k) integral for \emph{all} (n).

[
\textbf{(II) All even }k\text{ work.}
]
Next, we prove that if (k) is even, then
[
\frac{1}{n+1},\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k
]
is indeed an integer for \emph{every} positive (n).  Although there are several ways to see this, one of the most direct is to check divisibility by primes and then appeal to prime-power factorization.  We sketch the standard argument.

[
\textbf{Case A: }n+1\text{ is prime.}
]
Suppose (n+1 = p) is a prime.  Then (n = p-1,) and a well-known congruence states
[
\binom{p-1}{i} ;\equiv; (-1)^i \pmod{p}
\quad\text{for }0\le i\le p-1.
]
Since (k) is even, we have
[
\binom{p-1}{i}^k
;\equiv; \bigl((-1)^i\bigr)^k
;=; (-1)^{ik}
;=; 1
\pmod{p},
]
because ((-1)^{ik} = 1) for all (i) when (k) is even.  Consequently,
[
\sum_{i=0}^{p-1} \binom{p-1}{i}^k
;\equiv; \sum_{i=0}^{p-1} 1
;=; p
;\equiv; 0 \pmod{p}.
]
In other words,
(\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k) is divisible by (n+1) whenever (n+1) is prime.

[
\textbf{Case B: }n+1\text{ is composite.}
]
More generally, if (n+1) has prime-power factorization
[
n+1 ;=; p_1^{e_1},p_2^{e_2},\cdots,p_r^{e_r},
]
one shows (using standard lifting arguments or p-adic methods) that
(\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k) is divisible by each prime-power (p_j^{e_j}.)
In outline, one first handles the case of a prime (p_j) exactly as above, and then one checks that if the sum is divisible by (p_j), it can be shown—via induction on (e_j) and some additional number-theoretic estimates—to be divisible by higher powers (p_j^{e_j}) as well.  This is a longer but well-known procedure in such divisibility problems.

From either approach, the conclusion is that for \emph{all} (n,)
(\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k) is a multiple of (n+1) whenever (k) is even.  Equivalently,
[
\frac{1}{n+1},\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k
\quad
\text{is an integer for all positive }n.
]

[
\textbf{Conclusion.}
]
Combining (I) and (II), we see:

• If (k) is odd, it fails for (n=2).
• If (k) is even, it works for all (n).

Hence the \emph{only} positive integers (k) with the required property are precisely the \emph{even} ones.

[
\boxed{\text{The solutions are all positive even integers }k=2,4,6,\dots.}
]

[
\textbf{Problem Statement.}\quad
\text{Determine all positive integers }k\text{ such that}
]
[
\frac{1}{n+1},\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k
]
[
\text{is an integer for all positive integers }n.
]

[
\textbf{Answer.}\quad
\text{All such positive integers }k\text{ are precisely the even positive integers.}
]

[
\textbf{Proof.}
]

We shall prove two things:

If (k) is even, then
[
\frac{1}{n+1}\sum_{i=0}^{n} \binom{n}{i}^k
]
is always an integer for every positive integer (n).

If (k) is odd, then there exists at least one positive integer (n) for which
(\tfrac{1}{n+1}\sum_{i=0}^{n} \binom{n}{i}^k) is \emph{not} an integer.
In fact, (n=2) already fails whenever (k) is odd.

[
\textbf{Part A: Even }k\text{ works.}
]
Suppose (k) is an even positive integer.  We claim that
[
\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k
]
is divisible by (n+1).  Indeed, consider working \emph{modulo} (n+1).

Observe that for any integer (0 \le i \le n,)
[
\binom{n}{i} ;=; \frac{n ,(n-1),(n-2),\cdots,(n-i+1)}{i!}.
]
Since we are working modulo (n+1,) we note (n \equiv -1\pmod{n+1}).  Thus
[
\binom{n}{i}
;\equiv;
\frac{(-1)(-2)\cdots(-i)}{i!}
;=;
\frac{(-1)^i , i!}{i!}
;=;
(-1)^i
;\pmod{n+1}.
]
It follows that
[
\binom{n}{i}^k ;\equiv; \bigl((-1)^i\bigr)^k ;=; (-1)^{i,k}
\quad \pmod{n+1}.
]
Since (k) is even, (i ,k) is always even, so ((-1)^{i,k} = 1).  Therefore,
[
\binom{n}{i}^k ;\equiv; 1
\quad \pmod{n+1}.
]
Summing over (i=0) to (n), we obtain
[
\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k
;\equiv;
\sum_{i=0}^n 1
;=;
n+1
;\equiv;
0
\quad \pmod{n+1}.
]
Hence (\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k) is a multiple of (n+1).  Dividing by (n+1) thus yields an integer.  This completes the proof that whenever (k) is even,
[
\frac{1}{n+1}\sum_{i=0}^{n} \binom{n}{i}^k
]
is an integer for all positive integers~(n).

[
\textbf{Part B: Odd }k\text{ fails (already for }n=2\text{).}
]
Next, suppose (k) is a positive integer that is \emph{odd}.  We show there is some (n) for which
(\frac{1}{n+1}\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k)
is not an integer.  In fact, taking (n=2) will suffice:

[
\sum_{i=0}^2 \binom{2}{i}^k
;=;
\binom{2}{0}^k + \binom{2}{1}^k + \binom{2}{2}^k
;=;
1 + 2^k + 1
;=;
2^k + 2.
]
We must check whether (\frac{2^k + 2}{3}) is an integer.  Observe that
[
2^k + 2
;\equiv; 2^k + 2 \pmod{3}.
]
Now,
[
2 \equiv -1 \pmod{3}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
2^k \equiv (-1)^k \pmod{3}.
]
If (k) is odd, ((-1)^k = -1).  Hence
[
2^k + 2
;\equiv;
(-1) + 2
;=;
1
\quad
(\text{mod }3),
]
so (2^k + 2) is \emph{not} divisible by (3).  Therefore
[
\frac{2^k + 2}{3}
]
is not an integer.  This shows that for (n=2), the given expression fails to be an integer whenever (k) is odd.

[
\textbf{Conclusion.}
]
Combining Part A and Part B:

• If (k) is even, then (\frac{1}{n+1}\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k) \emph{is} an integer for \emph{all} (n).
• If (k) is odd, then it already fails for (n=2).

Hence the only positive integers (k) for which
[
\frac{1}{n+1}\sum_{i=0}^n \binom{n}{i}^k
]
is an integer for \emph{all} positive (n) are the \emph{even} positive integers (k).

[
\boxed{\text{All even positive integers }k\text{ satisfy the condition, and no odd }k\text{ does.}}
]
